window.harnessChunks["rows-c4d0aca87a6734e2.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T17:10:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HKUDS/AutoAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoAgent (HKUDS)</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HKUDS/AutoAgent/tree/16c12b052ef2330a198063c62a07a7f9723031e3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 16c12b052ef2330a198063c62a07a7f9723031e3\">16c12b0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous operational units directly transform task/environment state by browsing files/web resources or writing/executing code and return task outcomes into the AutoAgent run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user tasks, changing page/file state, code/runtime errors, tool results, and local environment observations that require different next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next environment-facing tool/action from current history/observations and decide when the assigned subtask is complete enough to return. - Decision owner: the active first-party AutoAgent specialist agent (File Surfer, Web Surfer, Coding Agent, or a generated plugin agent) through its model-driven decision loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MetaChain history/context propagation, tool dispatch, environment wrappers, registered tool sets, scoped workspaces, and deterministic transfer plumbing. - Closure path: observation/history → agent selects tool/action → first-party runtime executes it → result/changed environment returns into agent history → agent selects the next action or returns completed status to triage. - Boundary reachability: the specialist agents and MetaChain loop are shipped runtime components used by documented ready-to-use/user-mode execution; the positive path does not depend on repository-development or benchmark actors. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the specialist agent while retaining deterministic dispatch leaves no actor that chooses context-sensitive file/web/code actions; the runtime only executes the selected tool calls. - Evidence: autoagent/core.py, systemtriageagent.py, filesurferagent.py, websurferagent.py, programmingagent.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established; the reviewed multi-agent mechanisms do not identify a concrete interference, conflict, or oscillation among distinct S1 units that they specifically attenuate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: triage-to-specialist transfers, generated orchestrator handoffs, shared conversation/context propagation, asynchronous event groups, and workflow sequencing/joins. - Closure path: the mechanisms close delegation/workflow transitions, but no S2-specific disturbance → attenuation → changed-S1-behaviour loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents choose transfers and workflow actions, but autonomy over routing/delegation does not convert those mechanisms into S2 without the Profile&#39;s interference witness. - Evidence: systemtriageagent.py, editagents.py, flow/core.py, editworkflow.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control function is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task decomposition, specialist choice, construction errors, and workflow transitions are handled, but no evidenced current whole-system resource/commitment variety is regulated on behalf of the complete AutoAgent organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is shown holding a whole-system current view plus discretionary authority over shared resources, commitments, priorities, constraints, accountability, synergy, or intervention. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System Triage selects a specialist; generated orchestrators delegate subtasks and receive outputs; editor code retries failed parse/build/test attempts; the event engine schedules executable workflow events. - Closure path: those paths close task routing, construction, or workflow execution, not an S3 current-control conversation for the whole system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven triage/orchestration is agent-owned delegation, but the Profile explicitly requires more than worker selection/decomposition; no broader current-control decision right is evidenced. - Evidence: systemtriageagent.py, agentcreator.py, editagents.py, metachainmetaagent.py, flow/core.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary access-to-reality audit path is established inside the supported operating boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary construction errors and benchmark correctness are observable, but no operational claim is challenged through a materially independent audit channel that returns findings into current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool Editor runs newly created tools before success; Agent Creator runs created agents; workflow creation syntax-checks generated files; evaluation/ contains benchmark suites. - Closure path: editor tests can cause same-path retry/rework, but they are ordinary validation performed by the construction path itself; the repository evaluation suites are not wired into the declared standard-distribution runtime as a complementary audit/control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same editor/creator agent that creates an artifact also interprets its routine run/test outcome, so the evidence does not supply the complementary independence required for S3. - Evidence: tooleditor.py, metachainmetaagent.py, editworkflow.py, evaluation/. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt AutoAgent&#39;s reusable future capability from externally relevant task needs and available external tool/model capabilities, then return the selected adaptation into the installed runtime for later use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user capability requirements that current tools/agents do not satisfy, changing third-party API opportunities/documentation, and changing available model capabilities relevant to future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the concrete reusable adaptation—external capability/source, tool interface/implementation, required dependencies, agent composition, or workflow structure—and decide how to repair it until it passes execution/testing. - Decision owner: first-party meta-agents, especially Tool Editor and Agent Creator/Workflow Creator, operating autonomously inside the shipped editor modes. The user supplies the high-level requirement and may add suggestions, but the meta-agent selects and implements the concrete adaptation within that envelope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external API/model discovery tools; file creation; plugin decorators/registry; editable-package lookup; runtool, runagent, and runworkflow; deterministic parser/compile checks; bounded retry loops in the CLI. - Closure path: capability requirement → meta-agent inspects existing capability and, when relevant, external API/current model information → agent develops reusable tool/agent/workflow option → artifact is written into first-party plugin/workflow directories and registered → artifact is executed/tested → success leaves the capability available to subsequent agents/workflows/tasks in that installation. - Boundary reachability: Agent Editor and Workflow Editor are documented/shipped first-party operating modes reached through AutoAgent&#39;s CLI; the creation/test/registration loop runs against the installed editable AutoAgent package rather than a repository-development-only tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the Tool Editor/Creator agents while retaining file/registry/test utilities removes the actor that chooses which external capability to adopt and how to turn it into a reusable adaptation. Deterministic utilities write/test the selected option but do not make the adaptation judgment. - External distinction: the editor can query third-party API documentation and authentication details and search current trending Hugging Face models/capabilities when the requested tool requires them; user requirements also expose unmet environment-facing capability needs. - Future / prospective distinction: created tools are required to be abstract/modular/reusable rather than one-off task scripts; created agents/workflows are persisted as registered package artifacts intended for later execution beyond the current construction turn. - Adaptation option generated: reusable registered plugin tool, plugin agent/multi-agent orchestrator, or registered workflow plus any required dependencies. - Path back into current capability / S3: createtool/createagent/createworkflow write into AutoAgent&#39;s installed package; registration decorators expose the artifact through the global registry; runtool/runagent/runworkflow test and exercise it, after which later runtime calls can discover/use the new artifact. - Evidence: tooleditor.py, edittools.py, agentformer.py, agentcreator.py, workflowcreator.py, editworkflow.py, registry.py, metachainmetaagent.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: S4 credit is for the shipped editor/self-development mode, not ordinary user-mode task planning. A user can guide construction with requirements/suggestions, but the reviewed evidence does not establish a distinct parent-owned adaptation mode, so no (P) modifier is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the AutoAgent installation at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user requests, construction requirements, prompts, tool restrictions, and optional suggestions constrain operation, but no evidenced identity-level or ultimate-policy dispute is escalated to a legitimate authority and returned as governing policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system instructions, tool availability, protected built-in tools, user requirements/suggestions, environment scopes, and static framework constraints. - Closure path: ordinary instructions and constraints affect task/construction behavior directly; no separate identity/policy issue → ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither the agents nor a first-party parent mode are shown exercising ultimate identity/policy authority at this recursion. - Evidence: tooleditor.py, edittools.py, metachainmetaagent.py, systemtriageagent.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8783f1f0123f1d55eb13467918bc3c305bfe2d92/assessments/autoagent-hkuds.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8783f1f0123f1d55eb13467918bc3c305bfe2d92\">8783f1f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SamuelSchmidgall/AgentLaboratory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Laboratory</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SamuelSchmidgall/AgentLaboratory/tree/d9017d90e329112d2a80b7712f37ee9094d2cd27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d9017d90e329112d2a80b7712f37ee9094d2cd27\">d9017d9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform environment-facing research work across data preparation, executable experimentation, interpretation and report production. - Disturbance / variety regulated: data/source-selection problems, generated-code errors, experiment outcomes, model responses, interpretation uncertainty and report-generation needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select context-sensitive data/code/experiment/research actions within each operational phase and revise them from execution/dialogue feedback. - Decision owner: specialized first-party agents and solver agents (notably ML/SW/PhD/postdoc paths) invoked by LaboratoryWorkflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: phase loop, code execution feedback, MLESolver, PaperSolver, max-step bounds, shared accepted products and checkpoint state. - Closure path: phase goal/current products -&gt; specialized agent proposes action/code -&gt; first-party tools/solvers execute/materialize -&gt; error/result/dialogue returns -&gt; agent revises or submits phase product. - Boundary reachability: these agents and solvers are instantiated by the documented ailabrepo.py workflow invoked from the supplied YAML entrypoint. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workflow code sequences phases, but local scientific/engineering choices are made by model-driven specialized agents. - Evidence: README.md, ailabrepo.py, agents.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: specialized agents exchange dialogue and products, but no concrete cross-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation is identified and attenuated through a coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: phase sequence, ML/SW dialogue passing, shared notes/products and reset/checkpoint state. - Closure path: outputs/messages are passed to collaborators or next phases; no interference -&gt; attenuation decision -&gt; changed affected-S1 behavior loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: collaboration, role plurality and dialogue are not sufficient S2 evidence without a specific interference-regulation witness. - Evidence: ailabrepo.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no qualifying whole-system current-control owner is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workflow phases, retry loops, max-step budgets and report-refinement decisions alter progression, but whole-run control is predominantly scripted/configured and local phase judgments do not amount to broad S3 authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-project current view and discretionary authority over shared resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: self.phases, phasestatus, solver/max-step budgets, checkpoint state, recursive reset to earlier phases and Co-Pilot flags. - Closure path: scripted phase state/flags govern progression; local agent submissions or human phase acceptance feed that lifecycle without establishing S3&#39;s whole-system decision right. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specialized agents make local research decisions, while workflow-wide progression and resets are encoded in Python/YAML or depend on local review controls. - Evidence: ailabrepo.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: a material independent review mechanism exists, but standard-mode corrective closure is ambiguous at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: manuscript/experiment weaknesses, soundness/presentation/contribution/originality concerns and other peer-review objections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: three separately prompted reviewer calls produce critical review judgments; a downstream branch can in principle choose to return the project to planning/experimentation. - Decision owner: ReviewersAgent owns review judgments; potential corrective-return choice is intended for a PhD/human path but is not cleanly established as an enabled first-party standard mode in the pinned code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: three reviewer prompts, structured conference-style scoring, reportrefinement(), stored reviewerresponse, phase-status reset and recursive performresearch(). - Closure path: report -&gt; three reviewer judgments -&gt; reportrefinement; code contains a y branch that resets plan/experiment/report phases and re-enters research with reviewer feedback, but constructor-level reviewoverride = True with zero default review steps forces the automated branch to n (complete) unless runtime state is altered. - Boundary reachability: reviewer generation is directly reachable; the autonomous corrective return is present in first-party code but its supported standard-mode reachability is not documented/established strongly enough for A or C under Methodology 0.3.5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic review is clearly agent-owned, but the decisive closure from independent findings back to corrective operation is the uncertain part. - Claim being audited: that the resulting report/experiments constitute an adequate scientific submission. - Ordinary reporting path: experiment results/interpretation -&gt; PaperSolver report. - Complementary access path: ReviewersAgent invokes three distinct critical reviewer personas over the plan and completed report. - Independence boundary: reviewer invocations are separate from the operational PhD/ML/SW/PaperSolver generation path. - Who acts on findings: potentially the research workflow after report-refinement reset; default pinned reachability of that action is unresolved because of reviewoverride behavior. - Evidence: agents.py, ailabrepo.py. - Basis: structural -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: acquire external scientific evidence, synthesize future research plans and return those plans into experimentation, with both autonomous and human Co-Pilot review modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new/related literature, external AgentRxiv work, alternative experimental plans and human judgments that the proposed literature synthesis or plan should change before execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in base mode, PhD/postdoc agents search/select/summarize literature and formulate the experiment plan; in Co-Pilot mode, the human can reject a phase product, provide notes and force regeneration under those notes. - Decision owner: PhD/postdoc research agents in autonomous mode; human researcher in the documented Co-Pilot parent mode for reviewed literature/plan decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: arXiv/AgentRxiv search tools, literature-review accumulation, plan-formulation dialogue, phase-specific notes, YAML copilotmode, humaninloop() phase rejection/retry and shared agent state. - Closure path: external literature/topic -&gt; agents synthesize literature and plan -&gt; autonomous acceptance or human Co-Pilot review -&gt; rejected output is reset and human notes are attached to that phase -&gt; revised literature/plan -&gt; experimentation consumes the accepted plan/data context. - Boundary reachability: autonomous literature/plan phases are standard workflow stages; copilotmode is documented and parsed from shipped YAML, and humaninloop() is called directly from literature/plan phases. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: base research-option synthesis is agent-owned; parent mode intentionally transfers phase acceptance/adaptation judgment to the human and returns their notes into the next agent attempt. - External distinction: arXiv/AgentRxiv papers, dataset/search context and human research-domain feedback. - Future / prospective distinction: which literature matters and what experiment plan should be attempted next. - Adaptation option generated: literature synthesis and an explicit research/experiment plan used by downstream data preparation/experimentation. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted or human-revised plan and literature summaries are copied into agent/solver state and directly condition subsequent experimental execution. - Evidence: README.md, ailabrepo.py, agents.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: (P) is limited to documented Co-Pilot phase review/retry. Merely supplying initial task notes would not be enough for the parent modifier.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: research topic, notes, compute/model constraints and Co-Pilot approvals shape the project but do not constitute an identity-level policy issue with ultimate-authority closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML config, task notes, human phase-review flags, model selection and compute/max-step parameters. - Closure path: configuration/human notes constrain task execution directly; no identity/ultimate-policy dispute -&gt; authority -&gt; returned governing decision loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: professor/PhD role labels do not supply S5 authority, and human Co-Pilot review remains phase/task-level guidance. - Evidence: README.md, ailabrepo.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/agent-laboratory.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T23:43:37+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lastmile-ai/mcp-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mcp-agent</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lastmile-ai/mcp-agent/tree/f62d849350816588b1c6294e7914bbe4d8b84072\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f62d849350816588b1c6294e7914bbe4d8b84072\">f62d849 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-specified task work using model-driven tool-using agents and produce task evidence/artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous task demands, MCP tool results, errors, content differences and model-generated action choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each task agent&#39;s configured LLM chooses the response and permitted MCP tool use; a model-backed agent designer may generate role/tool instructions for an unmatched task. - Decision owner: instantiated Agent/AugmentedLLM in task execution, with permitted MCP servers and model providers furnishing external capabilities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK agent/session lifecycle, server registry, retry loop, task context builder, executor and result store. - Closure path: objective/task context → LLM/tool execution → generated answer, artifacts or error → stored TaskResult → next tasks/final response. - Boundary reachability: public createdeeporchestrator factory constructs a DeepOrchestrator; its TaskExecutor.executetaskonce attaches an LLM to the selected/dynamically created Agent and awaits generatestr. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed task actors make semantic choices about task responses and tool use; Python and MCP transport schedule/enforce rather than supply those choices. - Evidence: workflows/factory.py (createdeeporchestrator), taskexecutor.py (executetask, createdynamicagent), agents/agent.py. - Basis: explicit/structural. - Confidence: high for source-wired agent path; outcomes are not independently run. - Caveats: depends on caller-supplied models/servers/credentials and the selected workflow; no claim every mode is multi-agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated feedback controller specifically attenuating interference or oscillations among distinct concurrently operating S1 units at the chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate tasks, dependency constraints, parallel fanout and token-context limits are addressed as ordinary execution/ordering concerns; no source witness of mutually interfering S1 operational claims is supplied. - Decisive decision or feedback right: TodoQueue deduplicates names/task hashes and TaskExecutor awaits sibling tasks; neither consumes a particular operational conflict signal and returns mediation to both actors. - Decision owner: deterministic queue/executor and initial model planner, not an independently evidenced inter-S1 disturbance attenuator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan dependency verifier, seen-task sets, sequential steps, executor parallel fanout, shared context and cache. - Closure path: planner produces nonduplicated queued steps → agents execute → queue records completion; no inter-agent collision → mediator → modified independent operating behavior loop shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model task planning and coordination syntax do not establish S2 autonomy without a specific cross-unit interference mechanism. - Evidence: queue.py (mergeplan, filterstep), planverifier.py, taskexecutor.py (executestep). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for examined shipped multi-agent workflow. - Caveats: an application built on mcp-agent may implement its own S2; such user code is outside this library review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: observe cumulative present workflow progress and exercise whole-objective control over the next assignment, replanning, continuation and termination of subordinate S1 tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete coverage of the user objective, failed subtasks, exceeded resources, invalid proposed plans and fragmented task knowledge. - Whole-system current view: TodoQueue retains pending/completed steps and task states; WorkspaceMemory stores task results/knowledge/artifacts; SimpleBudget tracks aggregate token/cost/time use; planner and ObjectiveVerifier receive cumulative progress/knowledge. - Current-control decision scope: model StrategicPlanner chooses full next-step/task allocations and revised plans; model ObjectiveVerifier reports completion with confidence; PolicyEngine and configured limits enforce continue/replan/force-complete/emergency-stop; those decisions directly change live queue/execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agents propose and assess whole-objective plans; the runtime verifies proposals, loads or merges their steps and applies configured stopping/continuation decisions. - Decision owner: model-backed StrategicPlanner and ObjectiveVerifier for semantic whole-run judgments, constrained by deterministic plan validator/policy, with objective supplied by the operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PlanVerifier, TodoQueue, WorkspaceMemory, SimpleBudget, PolicyEngine, final synthesis and iteration/replan caps. - Closure path: aggregate task feedback → whole-objective planner/verifier judgment → policy action → rewritten/merged execution queue or terminal synthesis → updated task results and progress. - Boundary reachability: optional factory-created DeepOrchestrator.generate calls executeworkflow; inside its loop, empty-queue completion verification and policy-directed createfullplan/mergeplan are live paths, not standalone unused utilities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model planning and objective verification supply semantic whole-run decisions, while code deterministically constrains budgets and executes those decisions; model ownership is conditional on deep-orchestrator mode. - Evidence: orchestrator.py (executeworkflow, createfullplan, verifycompletion), policy.py, budget.py, memory.py. - Basis: explicit/structural. - Confidence: medium-high for implemented conditional path, not a measured end-to-end success guarantee. - Caveats: structural plan verification returns a final invalid plan with a warning after exhausted retries, and reported completion can be model-dependent; do not infer robust fail-closed task success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated independent, complementary audit of actual operations capable of challenging S3&#39;s inside-and-now view through a separate supervisory feedback channel. - Disturbance / variety regulated: inaccurate completion claims and invalid plans receive routine verification, not independently directed spot inspection of operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ObjectiveVerifier receives the same queue summary, knowledge and artifacts and triggers ordinary completion/replan policy; PlanVerifier performs static plan checks. - Decision owner: workflow&#39;s normal model verifier and deterministic validator, both inside the execution-control loop, not an independently chartered audit actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: confidence threshold, plan errors and generated task summaries. - Closure path: completion verification feeds the routine policy decision; there is no separate audit sampling of real actions with challenge/correction to S3 oversight. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: separate model role alone is insufficient for independent complementary audit at this organizational boundary. - Evidence: orchestrator.py (verifycompletion), planverifier.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: downstream apps may configure external evaluators, excluded unless they are installed first-party audit authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party prospective intelligence loop that studies changes in the external operating environment and returns capability-level adaptation options for later horizons. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unforeseen subtasks and failed task attempts trigger within-objective replanning, which supports current delivery instead of adjusting the workflow&#39;s future identity, architecture or operating capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model StrategicPlanner updates the current task plan from previous results; it does not install a revised strategy/tool policy for future independent objectives based on externally observed trends. - Decision owner: present-workflow model planner and configured operator; no separately continuing future-facing intelligence owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: replan count, knowledge extraction, context builder and active queue merge. - Closure path: current task failures/progress → replan current objective → continue same job; no outside horizon → prospective adaptation option → governance/control acceptance → changed future capability return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agentic replanning is real, but the evidenced decision right is S3 current control, not prospective S4 adaptation across a future environment. - Evidence: orchestrator.py (createfullplan, executeworkflow), knowledge.py, contextbuilder.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for inspected standard workflow, without asserting inability of downstream applications to add S4. - Caveats: project documentation calls it adaptive; this is current task adaptation, a narrower function than VSM S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party agent with legitimate ultimate-authority loop that deliberates or ratifies the organization&#39;s mission, values and governing operating envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool limits, user objectives, model settings, token/cost budgets and retry thresholds are constrained by operator configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the external user chooses objective, tool access, models and budgets; internal agent can propose task plans but cannot legitimately change those governing commitments. - Decision owner: external app operator and SDK config, not an authorized self-governance or parent-policy actor in the running workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DeepOrchestratorConfig, PolicyEngine, SDK app lifecycle, CLI/deployment interfaces. - Closure path: fixed operator policy limits agent execution; no identity-level issue → legitimate S5 ratification → returned change of operative purpose is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task allocation and resource shutdown rights do not confer authority to set ultimate system identity. - Evidence: config.py, policy.py, workflows/factory.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded first-party library. - Caveats: OSS maintainer governance and application-user instructions are outside actors, not internally evidenced (P) return paths.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4a08e5887a130e6181ee2c7ea9dfa6626a3c26b3/assessments/mc/mcp-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4a08e5887a130e6181ee2c7ea9dfa6626a3c26b3\">4a08e58 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T20:18:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/graniet/kheish\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kheish</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/graniet/kheish/tree/df50433f8d577f832c6812b9e67be8ddb766059d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision df50433f8d577f832c6812b9e67be8ddb766059d\">df50433 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user/project tasks by model-selected tool actions, revisions and outputs through durable agent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous goals, environmental/tool feedback, interrupted processes, provider failures and variable execution steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next task/tool action after model output and previous observation, or request further help within the session scope. - Decision owner: configured model-backed root and sidechain agents; the daemon enforces execution, persistence and credentials rather than selecting task-specific solutions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentRuntime, AgentOrchestrator, scoped ToolRuntime, permission engine, run journals and resume queues. - Closure path: submitted session input → model-selected call → tool result or gate/interrupt → persisted observation/resumed turn → model revises or finishes. - Boundary reachability: daemon serve plus configured provider and normal session/run API create the shipped execution path; sidechains and their narrowed context are spawned by first-party supervisor APIs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: open-ended tool and next-step selection rests with the model loop; fixed queues and policy merely limit that discretion. - Evidence: README quickstart; runtime.rs, orchestrator.rs, supervisor.rs; docs/concepts/agents-and-sidechains.mdx. - Basis: structural model/tool/observation lifecycle and explicit supported daemon entry. - Confidence: high for architecture, not for live task performance. - Caveats: provider credentials are required for a real model run; no paid-provider task was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate duplicate execution of one shared project commitment and invalid concurrent starts while separate session agents pursue project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: more than one actor attempting to start the same assigned project task, stale assignment during preparation, or a dependent work cell starting before its predecessor&#39;s completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit only a task start whose assignee/dependencies are still valid, and reject a second nonterminal start for that shared commitment. - Decision owner: constructor-authored project service reservation/dependency rules, not the model that submits a start request. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable project/member/task store, blockedby, expected-assignee compare, latest-run correlation, and session run queue. - Closure path: shared task state → reservation/revalidation → one eligible assignee run starts or competing/unready request is rejected → later project task state and dependency completion determine subsequent starts. - Boundary reachability: project service and start API are shipped daemon features and use the same persisted normal session-run path; no external scheduler or ad hoc user lock is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: rule-based start arbitration is constructor-owned; the project coordinator can still choose work, but these collision gates do not delegate the arbitration rule to a model. - Distinct S1 units: separately configured project session members, each with a managed model/tool runtime; a project task has one assignee session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous attempts to act on the same project task and downstream work using unfinished or reassigned output risk duplicate/conflicting execution. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic start reservation, nonterminal duplicate-start rejection, assignment revalidation and completed-dependency requirement. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected starts do not generate a second run; successful run state updates the project task so dependent session work can become eligible. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the gates handle a concrete shared-work collision and prerequisite disturbance across separately executing sessions, changing actual run eligibility, not merely moving messages. - Evidence: services/project.rs reservation and dependency tests; state/projectworkflow.rs; docs/concepts/projects-and-project-tasks.mdx. - Basis: structural concurrency control in first-party daemon code. - Confidence: medium; bounded to project-task start contention and declared dependencies. - Caveats: this does not prove general conflict avoidance for agents editing the same external file or that every project elects this mode.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: monitor and revise current project/session work commitments across delegated task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missed subtasks, changing current priorities, failed children, assignment bottlenecks and incomplete work dependencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coordinating model chooses subtasks/owners, reads current status/results, and updates or reassigns active commitments through control tools. - Decision owner: model executing the first-party coordination specialist profile at the declared group level; a human/project operator can separately direct or override it, but ordinary model-controlled current work is the credited mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listtasks, updatetask, child spawn/messaging, stored project/session task state, permission and dependency gates; the daemon enforces what the agent chooses. - Closure path: coordinator sees active task/child state → directs or revises assignment and follow-up → service validates/persists change → affected session agent receives task/mailbox work or subsequently eligible project start. - Boundary reachability: coordinator profile is shipped in helpers.rs and is executable as a managed agent with exposed task and sidechain control tools; its decisions affect the same daemon sessions/project execution boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model, not the deterministic task store, decides which commitment needs revision; the store implements authorized state change. - Whole-system current view: the selected project or session group&#39;s task list, assignees, dependency/status records and returned child snapshots/messages, not a claim to control unrelated daemon tenants. - Current-control decision scope: active group task decomposition, ownership/status revisions, follow-up and interruption/stop within configured capability scope; no ultimate organizational policy authority. - Evidence: controltools/helpers.rs coordinator instructions; controltools/tasks.rs list/update, orchestration and mailboxes; services/project.rs task state; docs/concepts/agents-and-sidechains.mdx. - Basis: explicit packaged model role plus the corresponding executable state/feedback tools. - Confidence: medium; reachable configurable coordinator mode, not a demonstrated unattended deployment. - Caveats: external model/provider and appropriate rights are required; a simple single-session deployment may not exercise group-level S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: complementary independent challenge to ordinary operating/current-control reports, beyond routine task checking. - Disturbance / variety regulated: inaccurate agent self-reporting, concealed execution defects and misleading task completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independently sourced audit judgment that can challenge current organizational control. - Decision owner: no sufficiently independent shipped audit decision owner established for the declared group; ordinary run and Flow reviewers remain part of configured task execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only event journals, signed external-action records, debug captures, evidence-required Flow projections and optional verification sidechains. - Closure path: review/evidence may fail a Flow or send a child finding to its parent task, but no distinct complementary audit channel with separate access and S3 correction is mandated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: logs, routine review and separation of a tool profile are not by themselves independent organizational audit judgment. - Evidence: supervisor.rs audit entries; docs/concepts/playbooks-and-flows.mdx; docs/concepts/agents-and-sidechains.mdx. - Basis: boundary and independence comparison. - Confidence: medium-high within sampled shipped paths. - Caveats: an application may compose its own truly independent auditor, but that is not attributed to the default first-party group.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: prospective intelligence that models the changing external environment, forms adaptation options and returns them to current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future provider/ecosystem changes, changing requirements and long-horizon operational risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluate future environmental possibilities and authorize candidate capability adaptations. - Decision owner: no shipped first-party prospectively oriented S4 owner established; optional model learning judges and operators review past run knowledge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: semantic fact/preference/decision extraction, scoped retrieval, optional judge, verified publication tiers and reviewed procedure skill rollout. - Closure path: terminal run → learning candidate/review → future prompt memory or separate operator skill promotion; this is retrospective knowledge reuse, not an external-prospective S3–S4 conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive memory and procedure activation can change later prompts, but no distinct future-environment model and option-return loop was found. - Evidence: docs/concepts/semantic-learning-and-procedural-skills.mdx; services/learning.rs, services/learningpolicy.rs and services/learningjudge.rs. - Basis: documented feature modes contrasted against Profile prospective requirement. - Confidence: medium-high for reviewed first-party learning modes. - Caveats: users may supply a future-scanning agent as an application, not a credited bundled organizational S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: legitimate ultimate identity/mission/policy adjudication at the project/daemon recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: contested operating purpose, credential risk, permission exceptions and conflicts between operational need and identity boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle a genuinely identity-level issue and return an authoritative policy decision to govern subsequent organization. - Decision owner: external operator/installer defines purpose, permissions, provider credentials and runtime configuration; no internally reachable identity-policy decision and return cycle is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: brokered auth leases, inherited non-widening child scopes, approval/user-question APIs, static runtime policy, Playbook release states and audit trail. - Closure path: an operator can configure/approve a task and execution resumes, but the reviewed standard product does not distinguish and route an ultimate identity issue through first-party S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission and human-input gates restrict individual actions; they are not authority to redefine the system&#39;s identity or settle S3–S4 tensions. - Evidence: README security; docs/concepts/approvals-and-questions.mdx, docs/runtime/permissions.mdx, docs/concepts/playbooks-and-flows.mdx and runtime auth/permission code. - Basis: explicit operator-rights boundary and absence of a policy-level escalation contract. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a wider organization deploying Kheish can supply its own legitimate parent S5, but no separate first-party P loop is evidenced at this chosen boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ba989f8be97bd7c592b6e9e969e185562b9814d4/assessments/kh/kheish.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ba989f8be97bd7c592b6e9e969e185562b9814d4\">ba989f8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T20:07:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tcztzy/swarmx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SwarmX</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tcztzy/swarmx/tree/a171e0da84c7a4d667d2c314cfbed66847f3040b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a171e0da84c7a4d667d2c314cfbed66847f3040b\">a171e0d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce goal-directed work through native agent tool loops and retained responsibility for delegated results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-specific uncertainty, failing/partial child results, native errors and differing capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select operational actions, delegation and corrective follow-up until a goal is ready for independent acceptance or escalation. - Decision owner: The configured native executor or managed supervisor Agent; the user separately owns criteria and final acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProductServices native adapters, recorded execution, preparation checks, WorkManager reservations and cancellation propagation. - Closure path: runWork prepares goal/criteria → native supervisor/executor → child/tool results → agent judges next operational action → returned output/submission. - Boundary reachability: runWork explicitly injects managed supervisor instructions and dispatches configured native Agents; source exposes and checks swarm.prepare/sendmessage. Native dependencies and credentials are explicit supported setup, not borrowed developer agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Host does not invent the next corrective message; removing the native agent&#39;s judgments leaves dispatch/recording without the operational response choices. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts lines 531–609 and 800–891; packages/core/swarm/skills/delegate/SKILL.md; docs/native-agents.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the wired supported mode. - Caveats: No live native model/provider run was executed. Operational completion is not independent acceptance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No material first-party function established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Interference between concurrent work agents or review consumers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive-return path supplied. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner; operational agent choices, caller settings and runtime enforcement remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cycle concurrency/reservations and directory-scoped review locks attenuate concrete duplicate consumption and overspending, but the response is fixed Host/SQLite enforcement; no first-party agent coordination decision or S2-specific autonomous composition contract is exposed. - Closure path: Positive, function-specific constructor and eligible parent-mode alternatives checked; no qualifying closure remains. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The bounded absence concerns the organizational relation, not implementation purity. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/work.ts; docs/memory.md; apps/desktop/tests/memory-review-ownership.test.ts - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the assessed distribution. - Caveats: A custom deployment could add the function; that is outside this shipped boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate current whole-cycle commitments, resources and accountability across persistent work items. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing goals, held/spent/unknown costs, unresolved effects, limited capacity and changing accepted commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Set/revise cycle budget and goal criteria, start/stop/revise work, reconcile unresolved outcomes/invoices and record independent acceptance. - Decision owner: The legitimate local operator through trusted desktop/Host administration; active Agents are explicitly denied this authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkManager transactions, expected-version checks, retained reservations, descendant accounting and authenticated window IPC. - Closure path: Cycle snapshot exposes item/attempt/spending/feedback state → operator revises commitments/budget or reconciles outcome → stored state changes → reserve/runWork obeys the returned limits and accepted dependencies. - Boundary reachability: Long-term work desktop and trusted Host APIs are first-party shipped controls. WorkManager.trusted rejects administration/acceptance from active agent sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A supervisor controls one work item&#39;s execution, not the declared whole-cycle budgets/criteria. Removing operator choices leaves fixed admission rules, not autonomous whole-cycle regulation. - Whole-system current view: Cycle snapshot combines items, attempts, feedback, spend, reservations and unknown coverage at one directory/cycle. - Current-control decision scope: Whole-cycle budget, commitments/criteria, acceptance, continuation and reconciliation; P is a complete supported operator mode. - Evidence: docs/work-management.md; apps/desktop/src/host/work.ts lines 155–205, 340–438 and 538–650; apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts runWork. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No whole-cycle S3=A is inferred from single-item supervisor delegation or hard concurrency/budget gates.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material first-party function established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Independent challenge to routine operational correctness/acceptance claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive-return path supplied. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner; operational agent choices, caller settings and runtime enforcement remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trusted user/validator feedback is the routine production acceptance path. Memory reviews receive journal snapshots without tools; registered candidate evaluators test proposed resource updates rather than supply a separately governed sporadic operational audit. - Closure path: Positive, function-specific constructor and eligible parent-mode alternatives checked; no qualifying closure remains. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The bounded absence concerns the organizational relation, not implementation purity. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/memory-review.ts; docs/learning-resources.md - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the assessed distribution. - Caveats: A custom deployment could add the function; that is outside this shipped boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Adapt future execution capability/selection to observed external harness/model/provider task fit and corrected user requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different provider/model/effort suitability, reliability/cost coverage and task conditions make an old selection policy or skill unsuitable for later work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Infer qualified future-use scenarios or prompt/skill changes from original execution and independent feedback, publish the chosen option into reusable selection knowledge; operator approval owns adoption in the alternate mode. - Decision owner: Base A: configured restricted Memory-review Agent chooses the adaptation content and Host applies validated plans. Parent P: authenticated local operator approves/rejects each staged option when writeApproval is enabled. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frozen original-event snapshots, evidence validation, durable plans, revision checks, optional fixed resource evaluator, pending-write UI and next-run preparation. - Closure path: External/native behavior and user correction → bounded reviewer judges task/provider/effort fit and proposes future selection/skill option → automatic validated publication or operator decision → later swarm.prepare loads that evaluated concept and current feedback into operational selection. - Boundary reachability: ProductServices constructs AgentMemory with reviewMemory and resumes it after native runtimes attach. Default Memory settings enable autoReview; the packaged delegate skill requires current private combination evaluations. Optional approval mode is wired through pending changes and decide/applyReview. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Credit is limited to explicit scenario-sensitive future combination/skill adaptation, not storage or retrospective summary alone. Removing reviewer judgment leaves snapshots/revision gates but no chosen future suitability recommendation. Parent adoption is separately owned when enabled. - External distinction: Observed external provider/harness/model/effort behavior, user feedback and cost/reliability conditions, retained with scoped versions/coverage rather than fabricated universal rankings. - Future / prospective distinction: The delegate skill asks which scenarios should choose a different combination next time; learned options persist across tasks and new preparations, rather than only repair the current answer. - Adaptation option generated: Evidence-backed agent-selection concepts/Playbooks and registered prompt/skill candidates with counterevidence and limitations. - Path back into current capability / S3: selection() reloads reviewed concepts plus current corrected feedback into swarm.prepare; operator-owned cycle grants/budgets bound adoption and subsequent work. Registered file changes apply on native next load. - Evidence: packages/core/swarm/skills/delegate/SKILL.md sections Choose a combination / Improve the evaluations; apps/desktop/src/host/memory.ts selection(), review prompt and applyReview; apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts AgentMemory construction and callSwarm prepare; apps/desktop/src/memory.ts; docs/learning-resources.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported selection-adaptation closure. - Caveats: No measured gain or general provider-quality claim. Only external/task-fit prospective adaptation is credited; ordinary memory writes and all generic self-improvement are not automatically S4. Learning disabled/grant-free deployments do not exercise this path.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No material first-party function established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Identity/ultimate-policy disputes at Host-cycle recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive-return path supplied. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner; operational agent choices, caller settings and runtime enforcement remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permissions, user notes, standing criteria and policy settings constrain work. updatePolicy requires idle execution and stores grants, but no identity-level dispute-to-legitimate-parent-and-return process is supplied. - Closure path: Positive, function-specific constructor and eligible parent-mode alternatives checked; no qualifying closure remains. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The bounded absence concerns the organizational relation, not implementation purity. - Evidence: apps/desktop/src/host/product-services.ts updatePolicy; docs/permissions.md - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the assessed distribution. - Caveats: A custom deployment could add the function; that is outside this shipped boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7f818d544b60c90b5684be978e78e0d34275ae9/assessments/sw/swarmx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7f818d544b60c90b5684be978e78e0d34275ae9\">e7f818d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-25T21:45:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genai-io/san\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">San</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genai-io/san/tree/dd1b41882472fb76ce38dc3f88107deffc2e73bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dd1b41882472fb76ce38dc3f88107deffc2e73bc\">dd1b418 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user software-engineering/terminal objectives into repository, filesystem, command, research or other tool-mediated outcomes through a repeated reason→act→observe loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository/environment state, ambiguous task requirements, tool output/errors, model responses, permission outcomes, context pressure and user feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive next model/tool actions and revise subsequent work from returned observations. - Decision owner: the active San main agent model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core run loop, tool registry, permissions/reviewer, session/transcript persistence, context management, provider adapters, skills/plugins/MCP and UI/runtime lifecycle. - Closure path: user objective enters session → model reasons and selects action/tool → San executes under policy → observation/result returns into conversation → model chooses next action from changed evidence → task/environment state changes and the cycle continues. - Boundary reachability: this is San&#39;s ordinary first-party interactive operating path, not a test-only or downstream composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves execution/policy machinery but removes the substantive choice of what work to do next and how to react to tool evidence. - Evidence: internal/core/run.go; internal/agent/build.go; internal/agent/session.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 relation is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying inter-S1 interference/oscillation witness is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S2 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent charters, bounded child execution, task/background machinery, session boundaries and concurrency protections can isolate/delegate work but do not by themselves establish coordination. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path reconstructs distinct credited S1 units → concrete inter-S1 disturbance → S2-specific attenuation → feedback changing subsequent S1 behaviour. - Distinct S1 units: the long-lived main session loop is the established S1. One-shot subagents are bounded delegated workers and are not credited as separate viable S1s at the parent recursion merely from process/session plurality. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established. - Attenuating coordination relation: not established beyond generic delegation/isolation/execution boundaries. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: not established for an S2-specific relation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is deliberately not mapped as S2; the reviewed evidence supplies delegation and worker isolation, not the Profile&#39;s complete inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no S2 function is established, so ownership classification does not proceed. - Evidence: internal/subagent/executor.go; docs/concepts/harness-channels.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current San session&#39;s mission-level commitment and continuation between operational turns, deciding whether present work should continue autonomously, terminate as accomplished, or return to human authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: locally completed turns that leave the mission incomplete, step-limit/truncation stops, recoverable failures, uncertain next steps, completed missions and situations that genuinely require a human-only decision/access grant. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose continue, done, or hand-back and, for continue, author the concrete next instruction sent back to the main agent. - Decision owner: the separate AutoPilot LLM judge/copot actor when AutoPilot mode is engaged. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted AutoPilot mission/config, recent-transcript/evidence renderer, continuation/recovery budgets, stop-reason gates, UI submit path and deterministic validation of structured decision states. - Closure path: main S1 turn ends or stops resumably → AutoPilot receives mission plus recent whole-session evidence → AutoPilot chooses continue/done/hand-back → on continue its instruction is submitted as the next visible input to the main agent; on done the mission is retired; otherwise control returns to the user → subsequent current operation changes accordingly. - Boundary reachability: AutoPilot is a first-party session mode wired through the San app and can be started/configured from the product rather than requiring downstream code. - Whole-system current view: the controller receives the mission and bounded recent session evidence including conversation, compact summaries and tool outcomes, and is explicitly instructed to judge what is already accomplished from all supplied evidence rather than only the last turn. - Current-control decision scope: the session&#39;s current commitment to unattended mission execution—continue with a new concrete step, stop as complete, or escalate control back to the human. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: budgets/stop gates enforce safe boundaries, but they do not choose the mission-level continuation response or author the next instruction. Removing the AutoPilot model leaves those gates but removes the discretionary current-control judgment. - Evidence: internal/app/autopilot.go; internal/reviewer/reviewer.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary-audit closure is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: San exposes operational transcripts and replay state that could reveal mismatches, but no first-party independent audit judgment/control loop is established over those observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only transcripts, integrity/digest-aware replay, localhost read-only inspector, records/live-tail/state APIs and a permission reviewer. - Closure path: not applicable; the reviewed inspector path exposes operational reality but does not itself produce an independent corrective verdict that returns into runtime control. The permission reviewer acts before protected actions rather than auditing operational claims after/beside routine reporting. - Claim being audited: no qualifying first-party audit claim/loop established. - Ordinary reporting path: normal session messages/tool results/transcript records. - Complementary access path: inspector/replay can reconstruct raw transcript/replay state independently of the rendered assistant narrative. - Independence boundary: access is read-only and separate from the work loop, but access independence alone is insufficient without an audit judgment and feedback path. - Who acts on findings: not established as a first-party S3 closure; a human can inspect/debug, but generic human observation is not enough for the Methodology&#39;s autonomous audit state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no autonomous audit actor owns a complementary judgment. The pre-action permission reviewer owns a different function and is not reclassified as S3. - Evidence: internal/inspector/server.go; internal/inspector/replay.go; docs/packages/2-feature/inspector.md; internal/reviewer/reviewer.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: the inspector is a strong constructor/debugging surface from which an auditor could be composed, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not award C until the S3 function itself is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an external/prospective adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: self-learning can be triggered by the main model via Evolve; after a clean turn a restricted background fork reviews the completed conversation and can write durable project memory or agent-created skills that affect future sessions. - Closure path: not applicable for S4. The reviewed self-learning path closes retrospective experience consolidation into future memory/skills, but no first-party path reconstructs external/future-relevant sensing → development of adaptation options → S4 adaptation judgment → return into present capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model autonomously decides when a turn is worth learning from and the review fork can decide what lesson/skill to write, but autonomy plus learning is not sufficient. The Profile explicitly excludes generic learning/self-improvement unless it participates in an external-and-prospective adaptation loop. - Evidence: docs/packages/2-feature/selflearn.md; internal/selflearn/reviewer.go; internal/selflearn/prompts.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an identity/ultimate-policy matter. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5 within the standard session boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system-prompt identity/persona, rules, project/user memory, permission modes, AutoPilot mission/system prompt, reviewer decisions, skills and human approval constrain/configure operation but do not establish ultimate-policy closure. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed first-party path shows an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching legitimate ultimate authority and returning as authoritative policy governing subsequent San operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model actors operate inside configured identity, mission and permission boundaries; they are not shown exercising ultimate authority over those boundaries. Ordinary human permission or configuration edits likewise do not become S5 without an identity-level issue/decision/return loop. - Evidence: docs/concepts/harness-channels.md; internal/app/autopilot.go; internal/reviewer/reviewer.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: San deliberately exposes configurable identity/policy channels; Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat their existence as S5 without the identity/ultimate-policy function itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e4b2025677c98a7731c1bc46d6883f507f09c05f/assessments/san.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e4b2025677c98a7731c1bc46d6883f507f09c05f\">e4b2025 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T19:15:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Goose</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose/tree/50666ae0b9a51e260b52b7efbab2e4e020346e94\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50666ae0b9a51e260b52b7efbab2e4e020346e94\">50666ae &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the standard runtime autonomously selects and executes actions/tools within configured bounds and iterates from results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: no first-party S2 witness was established. The orchestrator permits top-level cross-session messages and permits delegated subagents to message siblings with the same parent, but generic communication does not identify a specific inter-S1 interference, conflict or oscillation together with a relation that attenuates it and feeds the result back into later S1 behavior. Subagent/fork support remains delegation rather than sufficient S2 evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the first-party orchestrator mode gives the autonomous top-level Goose agent a whole-system current view across sessions (listsessions / viewsession) and actual intervention authority over current commitments and operational capacity (startagent, sendmessage, interruptagent). These tools are directly model-visible and their effects are closed by the runtime; once the supported mode is enabled, a developer does not need to compose a separate autonomous actor, S3 decision authority, or closure loop. defaultenabled: false and hidden: true do not convert an otherwise closed agent-owned mode into C. The decisive evidence was already present at the accepted review ref, making this a same-ref correction rather than a new-ref architectural change.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no alternative, sufficiently independent complementary audit path with materially different access to operational reality was established. Session viewing is part of the same S3 current-control surface, not a distinct audit relation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: context management, retries and task reasoning concern current execution; no distinct externally and prospectively oriented intelligence loop that develops adaptation options and returns them into current capability was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: system prompts, permissions, extensions and runtime configuration remain developer/user supplied rather than an autonomous legitimate identity/ultimate-policy decision path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c18ecd44bdd2a63ce0893a114d25f5d3cc75a76a/assessments/goose.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c18ecd44bdd2a63ce0893a114d25f5d3cc75a76a\">c18ecd4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T18:08:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onelevenvy/flock\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Flock</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onelevenvy/flock/tree/2eac827aa803ae2ee8578e401a864e116cd994d3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2eac827aa803ae2ee8578e401a864e116cd994d3\">2eac827 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform environment-facing task work through an iterative model/tool loop and return resulting observations into subsequent action selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, changing local/project state, model responses, tool outputs/errors, sandbox/browser/process state and other execution observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action from current messages and tool results, then decide whether another operational action is needed or the task/run can terminate. - Decision owner: the active first-party Flock agent loop, including agent nodes used inside declarative workflows. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentEngine::run, Flock&#39;s modelrequest/calltools graph, tool registry/executor, sandbox creation, checkpoint state, retries/timeouts, iteration limits and workflow state transport. - Closure path: task/messages/environment state → agent model selects a tool/action → first-party Flock tool/runtime executes it → tool result or changed environment returns into agent messages/state → agent chooses the next action or terminates. - Boundary reachability: this is the core shipped flock-agent path used by ordinary runs; workflow agent nodes expose the same class of bounded autonomous tool loop in the supported declarative workflow surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic graph/tool machinery transports and enforces execution, but it does not choose the context-sensitive next operational action; removing the model-driven agent leaves no owner of that S1 discretion. - Evidence: crates/flock-agent/src/engine/run/run.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/graph/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established; workflows can contain multiple operational agent nodes, but the reviewed shipped mechanisms do not identify a specific actual or structurally evidenced interference, conflict or oscillation among those S1 units that a coordination relation is designed to attenuate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific actor is shown choosing or revising a coordination response to cross-S1 disturbance. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph edges, conditional routing, fan-out to multiple successors, shared workflow state, checkpointing, retries/timeouts and scheduler execution. - Closure path: these mechanisms move and sequence state/work among nodes, but no interference → attenuation decision → changed subsequent S1 behavior closure is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: individual agent nodes own their local operational choices; graph topology and scheduler semantics are deterministic/user-authored. Neither fact establishes autonomous coordination of a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/graph/mod.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control function is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workflow progress, node failures, retries, timeouts and branch routing are regulated mechanically, but no first-party actor is shown regulating current shared resources, commitments, priorities or constraints on behalf of the whole Flock organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped actor has both a whole-system current view and discretionary authority to revise relevant organization-wide commitments/resources/priorities/interventions. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow scheduler/compiled graph, checkpoints, retries/timeouts, cancellation state, workflow event sink, cron/headless execution controls and per-session approval state. - Closure path: these mechanisms close execution and error-handling transitions according to configured topology/policy, not a discretionary whole-system S3 control conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent nodes select local task actions, while the workflow compiler/scheduler enforces configured structure. Neither worker selection nor deterministic current-state execution supplies the broader S3 decision right. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/common.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/engine/run/run.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit path is established for the current S1/S3 control boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Flock exposes tool results, workflow events, retries and evaluation utilities, but no reviewed supported path independently challenges an operational unit&#39;s ordinary current-control claim and returns that finding into S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3 at the current-control layer. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow/tool event emission, retries, error state, checkpoints, logs, and grader/comparator/analyzer machinery bundled specifically inside the skill-development evaluation workflow. - Closure path: routine tool/workflow results feed the same operational path; skill-development graders/comparators feed S4 capability adaptation. Neither supplies a complementary current-operations audit channel returning findings to an S3 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent graders can judge skill-development trials, but the audited claim there is prospective adaptation quality rather than current S1 operational accountability; moving that mechanism across functions would be a category error. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, flock-data/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: develop and test reusable future Flock capability in response to capability needs and externally/future-relevant information, then return the resulting adaptation into the skill surface used by later operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a user&#39;s desired capability may not exist or an existing skill may perform poorly; relevant documentation, similar skills, best practices, edge cases, model/tool behavior, evaluation failures and changing triggering behavior create future-facing distinctions that should change the reusable capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the concrete skill design and revisions—workflow/instructions/resources, evaluation cases/assertions, changes suggested by trial transcripts/benchmarks, reusable helper scripts and triggering-description changes—then decide whether further adaptation iterations are warranted within the user&#39;s stated objective/feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party Flock agent following the shipped skill-creator adaptation procedure. The user/Parent supplies the capability goal, may confirm intent/evals and can stop or accept the loop, but the agent develops, tests and revises the adaptation options rather than merely persisting a user-authored artifact. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: packaged skill-creator instructions/resources; research through available tools/MCP where useful; with-skill versus baseline trial runs; graders/comparators/analyzer; benchmark aggregation; human feedback; description-optimization loop; filesystem skill format; skill loader/discovery and packaged application skill resources. - External distinction: relevant external documentation, similar skills, best practices, available tool/model behavior and user-environment requirements can expose capability gaps or better approaches that are not contained in the current skill alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the loop asks whether a reusable skill will handle representative future tasks and trigger appropriately, comparing candidate capability against baselines/previous versions and held-out or expanded evaluations rather than merely repairing the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: a new or revised reusable SKILL.md plus supporting scripts/resources, evaluation design and triggering description selected by the agent from those distinctions and trial results. - Path back into current capability / S3: the selected skill is written or packaged into Flock-readable user/project/workspace/install skill locations; flock-skills subsequently discovers/loads it into later agent contexts, changing the capability available to present operational runs. - Closure path: capability need + relevant external/future distinctions → agent researches/designs a reusable skill option → with-skill/baseline trials and feedback expose performance distinctions → agent revises the skill/description/resources → resulting skill is written/packaged in a Flock-readable skill location → later agent runs load/use the changed capability. - Boundary reachability: skill-creator is under flock-data/skills, those resources are included in the Tauri application bundle, and flock-skills resolves the installed skills resource directory plus user/project skill directories. This is a shipped product capability, not repository-only CI/dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: user intent and acceptance constrain the adaptation, but the substantive future-capability option generation, diagnosis of trial failures, revision choices and reusable artifact construction are delegated to the agent. Deterministic loaders/eval scripts support and measure the loop rather than owning those adaptation decisions. - Evidence: flock-data/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md, crates/flock-skills/src/paths.rs, crates/flock-skills/src/discovery.rs, flock-ui/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic loading/discovery of already-authored skills alone would not establish S4. Credit depends on the separately shipped adaptation procedure that actively researches/develops/tests/revises a capability and closes it back into the runtime-readable skill surface. Some evaluation branches depend on optional subagent/CLI facilities; the core draft → test/review → revise → persist loop remains explicitly adapted for environments without those facilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established at the reviewed Flock recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary tool risk and human intervention are regulated, but no evidenced organizational identity/policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority whose decision returns as non-routine global policy for subsequent operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolApprovalManager, per-session Default/AutoEdit/Yolo modes, per-category “always” approval, sensitive-tool approval, human nodes/ask-human/request-human-assistance, sandbox permissions and workflow configuration. - Closure path: human/tool approvals can approve or deny an ordinary pending action and session modes can change which tool categories auto-approve, but that is task/session authorization rather than identity/ultimate-policy closure balancing current operations and future adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval machinery enforces user/session choices; neither it nor an agent is shown owning legitimate ultimate organizational identity/policy authority. Ordinary human final say over a tool call does not meet S5 by itself. - Evidence: crates/flock-core/src/ipcinterface/approval.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, crates/flock-tools/src/tools/builtin/askhuman.rs, crates/flock-tools/src/tools/sandbox/requesthumanassistance.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c8624b55eaa098004a856ac116b7881495866f8f/assessments/flock.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c8624b55eaa098004a856ac116b7881495866f8f\">c8624b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T17:11:20Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xerrors/Yuxi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yuxi</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xerrors/Yuxi/tree/86c67dc41249c3c9cad9e976fd8a006194d39a76\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 86c67dc41249c3c9cad9e976fd8a006194d39a76\">86c67dc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed knowledge, analysis and artifact-producing work through a first-party model/tool execution loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, retrieved knowledge, files/workspace state, tool/API results, sandbox execution outcomes, model/tool errors and changing conversation/task context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive next actions/tools and decide when available operational evidence is sufficient to continue, delegate or return a result. - Decision owner: the running Yuxi model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Yuxi ChatbotAgent, configured runtime tools, composite filesystem/sandbox backend, checkpointer, request/run worker lifecycle, retries, context summarization, Skills/MCP and knowledge integration. - Closure path: persisted request → prepared Yuxi agent graph → model-selected tool/action → first-party execution → ToolMessage/observation → subsequent model decision → final persisted result. - Boundary reachability: the standard Yuxi API/worker path directly instantiates and executes ChatbotAgent; no downstream custom harness is required to obtain the model/tool operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LangGraph executes graph mechanics and external providers supply model inference, but the model actor owns the discretionary operational choices while Yuxi owns the executable loop, tools/context and durable run path. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/base.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/graph.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic LangGraph/DeepAgents capabilities not assembled and exposed by Yuxi are not inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes attenuation of a concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among at least two distinct Yuxi S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S2 at the reviewed recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child AgentRun lifecycle, same-thread no-concurrent-write rule, FIFO request queues, run leases, shared runtime/workdir, parent cancellation and delegation exist but are not credited as coordination by topology or serialization alone. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root can manage child commitments, but evidence inspected does not identify a concrete cross-child disturbance and an S2-specific attenuation relation whose feedback changes subsequent child behavior. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shared workspaces can create potential interaction, but potential collision is not evidence that Yuxi ships a concrete S2 attenuation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of subordinate Yuxi agent commitments during a root-agent run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete, delayed, failed, unnecessary or still-running subordinate tasks; changing need for specialist work as child results arrive; risk of finalizing while required delegated work remains unresolved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what child work to start, inspect current child status/progress, await results when needed, cancel a child, continue root work in parallel and condition subsequent commitments/finalization on current child state and returned results. - Decision owner: the root Yuxi model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Yuxi SubAgent middleware; durable parent/child AgentRun relation; subagentstart, subagentstatus, subagentcancel, subagentawait; ChatBotState.subagentruns; child ownership verification; cancellation/request services; persistent run/result state. - Closure path: root identifies current need → starts one or more configured subordinate operations → child run state/result returns as ToolMessage plus subagentruns state → root inspects/awaits/cancels/launches further work as current conditions require → subsequent root action/final answer changes accordingly; remaining live children are cancelled when parent ends. - Boundary reachability: standard configured ChatbotAgent construction automatically installs the SubAgent middleware whenever visible/configured Yuxi subagents exist; the root receives the current-control tools directly in its normal model loop. - Whole-system current view: the root graph state accumulates structured summaries for each child run keyed by runid, including current status, agent identity, timing/error/artifact/result fields; explicit status/await calls refresh individual commitments before the root acts. - Current-control decision scope: start new subordinate commitments, continue prior child threads when idle, inspect their progress/results, wait for required completion, cancel no-longer-wanted work and decide whether the root can safely finalize. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: repositories/services enforce ownership and durable state, but the root model decides which current commitments exist and how their live evidence changes ongoing portfolio action. Removing the model leaves lifecycle mechanics but no discretionary whole-run allocation/control decision. - Evidence: backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/graph.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/state.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py, ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this mapping is conditional on the standard supported mode with configured/visible SubAgents; a single-agent configuration still retains S1 but does not instantiate a multi-unit portfolio.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes a sufficiently independent complementary challenge to operational reality with corrective return into S1/S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/tool audit events, Langfuse integration, run/debug timelines, knowledge/RAG evaluation, human tool approval, retries and child status/results all exist but serve ordinary execution, evaluation or parent governance. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: child outputs are production inputs to the root, not a structurally independent audit channel; dashboard/evaluation evidence does not by itself exercise corrective operational authority; human approval is Parent intervention rather than S3. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/toolapproval.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a separately configured reviewer subagent could be constructed downstream, but role naming or constructibility is not a shipped independent audit closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes autonomous or parent-governed prospective environment-facing adaptation of Yuxi organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for prospective capability adaptation. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: knowledge/RAG retrieval, persistent checkpoints, user Memory, dynamically activated Skills, configurable models/tools/MCP and reusable agent/subagent definitions broaden available capability but do not themselves decide and integrate a future organizational adaptation from environmental distinctions. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the memory middleware explicitly writes long-term memory only when the user asks it to remember; Skills are selected/configured resources and dynamic activation occurs within the present run. Persistence/reuse is not prospective S4 closure. - Evidence: README.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/memory.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/skills.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: administrators/users can change knowledge, agent, Skill, MCP and model configuration, but generic external configuration does not establish Parent-governed S4 without a qualifying S4 issue/option/decision/return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes identity- or ultimate-policy-level adjudication that closes unresolved present/future tension for the Yuxi organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multi-tenant RBAC, administrator configuration, agent prompts/descriptions, tool approval mode, model/resource visibility and human approvals constrain operation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are access/configuration and parent safety controls. The reviewed repository does not establish a function-specific identity/ultimate-policy issue and an authority that adjudicates it and returns the decision into operation. - Evidence: README.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/toolapproval.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: super-admin/operator authority is real product governance but does not become VSM S5 without the identity/ultimate-policy organizational function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f0e739507a67e4069c7ef24add9a14d2bfcea7ec/assessments/yuxi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f0e739507a67e4069c7ef24add9a14d2bfcea7ec\">f0e7395 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T22:58:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AutoCodeRoverSG/auto-code-rover\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoCodeRover</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AutoCodeRoverSG/auto-code-rover/tree/585d3e639aeda58ef0b6a151dd1cc2721a94d267\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 585d3e639aeda58ef0b6a151dd1cc2721a94d267\">585d3e6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate a supplied code issue and produce an applicable candidate patch through autonomous search and code-edit choices. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty about code locations, incompatible search requests, non-applicable diffs, failed reproduction and regression outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select iterative search APIs and suspected bug locations, generate patch content, and revise attempts after code/tool feedback. - Decision owner: first-party search/patch model interactions; operator selects provider and issue, while deterministic backends execute code searches and validation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SearchBackend AST-aware calls, API-call proxy, diff extraction/applicability, optional SBFL and test execution, bounded retries and candidate selection. - Closure path: issue + repository → model-selected code search → returned source context → model-generated patch → applicability/test/reviewer feedback → next patch or output selection. - Boundary reachability: the main source-search/patch path is the default github-issue, local-issue and SWE-bench work mode and does not require the experimental reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated search instructions and patch content vary with observed task data; configured round ceilings, tool execution and user-provided issue are supporting restrictions, not owners of those decisions. - Evidence: search selects APIs and consumes outcomes, model-generated applicable patch and feedback, pipeline. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the shipped model/tool/patch control loop; no claim about the patch&#39;s external task success. - Caveats: live model-backed operation was not tested; ordinary validation is opt-in and some tasks fall back to patch-only generation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: in the experimental reproducer/reviewer mode, dampen conflict between independently proposed issue reproducer and corrective patch by adjudicating which of the two must be revised. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a patch can make a faulty test pass, or a valid patch can seem wrong because the test incorrectly represents the issue; indiscriminate iteration on only one cell would perpetuate mismatch. - Distinct S1 units: TestAgent autonomously proposes executable reproducing tests; PatchAgent autonomously proposes code changes, each with a dedicated model prompt, artifacts and feedback/revision history. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their joint candidate outcome disagrees with the observed buggy/patched reproducer results and issue semantics, with the conflict potentially attributable to either test construction or patch construction. - Attenuating coordination relation: an independent reviewer model is given the issue, both artifacts and original/patched execution evidence and returns separate patch-correct / test-correct judgments and targeted advice. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ReviewManager.generator routes patchdecision == NO to PatchAgent.addfeedback and writeapplicablepatchwithfeedback, while testdecision == NO updates TestAgent.addfeedback and writereproducingtestwithfeedback; the resulting artifacts are re-executed on the following round. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the reviewer is explicitly asked to settle which of two distinct operational outputs is defective, and that decision changes which S1 producer receives a corrective instruction. Merely executing tests or selecting the last patch is not the credited S2 relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dynamically judge whether to correct the test, patch or both from cross-cell evidence; the Python manager routes the judgment. - Decision owner: first-party model reviewer in the opt-in mode, not the fixed CLI scheduler or a user-assembled external supervisor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: original/patched repro execution, two-sided review JSON, separate feedback histories, bounded review rounds and validation. - Closure path: test + patch candidates → execute both interpretations → reviewer locates mismatch → affected producer receives focused feedback → regenerated test/patch enters next comparison. - Boundary reachability: the first-party SWE-bench --reproduce-and-review switch installs this loop when a valid reproducer exists; it is experimental, off by default, and not exposed for general local/GitHub issue commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the correctness allocation and advice are discretionary reviewer-model output; the manager&#39;s branches only implement the model&#39;s two judgments. This does not imply shared-system scheduling, generic writer-conflict mediation or whole-current governance. - Evidence: flag and scope, mode gate, two-sided reviewer prompt, feedback dispatched to each agent, reproducer feedback memory. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; the narrowly defined test/patch coordination closure is explicit, but no model-run has established effectiveness or a broader autonomous S2. - Caveats: opt-in experimental SWE-bench only; no reproducer means fallback to patch-only generation. The review loop is quality-focused, does not coordinate parallel workers or resource contention, and its S2 interpretation depends on treating the test and patch generators as distinct operational units rather than one monolithic coding S1.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: direct present resource, commitment, priority and accountability trade-offs of multiple operating units for the whole assessed harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/task retries, concurrent batch workloads, failing patch attempts and tool execution time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established at an S3-specific organizational level; task grouping, fixed retry limits and patch selection are preset process management. - Decision owner: human CLI/config author and deterministic orchestration, with model decisions local to repair tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProcessPoolExecutor, group sequencing, overall retry limit, model cycling, validation and generated patch selection. - Closure path: user-supplied task groups → parallel/serial processing → per-issue output; no separate current-wide negotiation/reprioritization and corrective allocation among an accountable whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: process limits and ranking candidate patches do not own whole-organization resource/commitment decisions. - Evidence: task group scheduling, bounded attempts and selection. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the bounded first-party negative. - Caveats: a separate operator organization can add current management, but is outside this assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independently challenge an S3 current-management claim using a complementary route to operational reality, then return findings for corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially false patch-correctness claims and misleading local tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such S3 audit right; the experimental agentreviewer decides whether local code/test artifacts are correct as ordinary repair-loop QA. - Decision owner: model reviewer and deterministic validator under S1/S2 repair production, not a distinct management auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: test execution against original/patched code, reviewer feedback JSON, optional regression validation and logs. - Closure path: code/test pair → reviewer/validator → revise task artifacts, not S3 claims → independent operational audit → S3 corrective management return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is model-owned but its object is the code patch and reproducer, not complementary verification of whole-current S3 management. - Evidence: reviewer&#39;s actual task, manager feedback scope, local patch validation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for task-level QA versus S3 boundary. - Caveats: the term &quot;reviewer&quot; alone does not establish audit ownership at organizational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: monitor wider future environment, propose adaptive changes and return selected options to current harness capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar future issue classes, external library changes, provider variation and changing codebases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no evidenced prospective organizational capability decision; model selection, fault localization and retrieval are for the present issue. - Decision owner: user/developers choose providers, install new methods and maintain the project; the repair model chooses present-task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model alternatives, AST search, issue context, regression feedback, retries and an external paper/benchmark. - Closure path: issue/code evidence → patch for the same issue, or configured provider fallback; not future trend sensing → strategic adaptation → returned capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: solving a previously unseen issue is S1 operational adaptation, not an installed outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: pipeline and model retry, public research and scope statement. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for reviewed runtime. - Caveats: external researchers can measure and change future versions, but their decisions are outside the one-run harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: settle ultimate purpose and binding identity/policy conflicts for the running organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requested edits outside intended scope, provider-policy rejection, task-local constraints and permissible execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party ultimate policy/identity decision path; user provides issues/models/options and model produces candidate patches subject to configured guards. - Decision owner: human operator/developer for values and configuration, with external provider policy enforced by provider. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI tasks, prompt instructions, errors/exception fallback and test validation. - Closure path: user-supplied goal/config → issue repair attempt; no identity-level disagreement → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy decision returned to the operating system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompt-level do/don&#39;t rules and fallback on an external content-policy exception do not transfer supreme policy authority to the coding agent. - Evidence: CLI goal and model selection, provider-policy exception fallback, patch prompt scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for bounded negative at the local runtime. - Caveats: broader project governance and legal restrictions are external arrangements.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3be8d80335ca7cbb047936f15ea0705c6d94f3dd/assessments/au/autocoderover.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3be8d80335ca7cbb047936f15ea0705c6d94f3dd\">3be8d80 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:41:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OS-Copilot/OS-Copilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OS-Copilot / FRIDAY</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OS-Copilot/OS-Copilot/tree/f720af8807e49a92dda64572d2c6bc6c0ac7ee7e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f720af8807e49a92dda64572d2c6bc6c0ac7ee7e\">f720af8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously plan and execute concrete computer/OS/application work needed to satisfy a user task, adapting task-local actions to returned execution state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, available tools, filesystem/application state, generated-code failures, missing intermediate operations, execution outputs/errors and changing prerequisites within a task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/decompose the next substantive computer actions, generate or select executable tools, interpret returned execution state and decide whether to continue, amend or replan the active task. - Decision owner: first-party model-driven FRIDAY planner/executor/judge path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool retrieval, dependency graph/topological ordering, Python/Shell/AppleScript/API execution environments, bounded repair iterations, task state, generated-tool repository and deterministic loop/control plumbing. - Closure path: user task -&gt; model decomposes/selects actions -&gt; generated/retrieved tool executes against OS/application environment -&gt; result/error/filesystem state returns -&gt; model judges/replans/repairs -&gt; changed action executes -&gt; task state updates. - Boundary reachability: quickstart.py and documented examples instantiate FridayAgent, while FridayAgent.run() directly invokes the credited planner/executor/refinement path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code transports state and executes selected code, while substantive task decomposition, tool generation, result judgment, amendment and replanning choices are model-driven. - Evidence: README.md; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the README notes single-round conversation, but the internal execution of one task is multi-step and result-conditioned; conversational turn count does not break the operational loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 path regulating a concrete disturbance between distinct autonomous S1 units is established at the declared FRIDAY-installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the planner orders dependencies among subtasks and modules exchange artifacts, but those subtasks/modules are components of one operational agent rather than separately accountable S1 units producing an inter-operation oscillation/interference problem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 coordination. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph, topological ordering, prerequisite-result passing, shared tool repository and sequential subtask loop. - Closure path: task decomposition creates ordered work inside S1, but no distinct S1-A/S1-B interaction disturbance -&gt; coordinating judgment -&gt; changed later behavior of those S1 units loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing dependencies is operational planning, not evidence of the VSM S2 organizational function. - Evidence: oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a deployment containing several separately accountable FRIDAY instances could create an S2 problem, but that is a different system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function over multiple or separately managed operational units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-local execution failures, weak outputs and missing prerequisites are regulated, but they are handled inside the same S1 episode that performs the work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate actor is evidenced with authority to form a whole-installation current view and intervene in the present operational organization beyond the active task-local plan/repair loop. - Decision owner: none established for S3; FRIDAY&#39;s planner/judge retains task-local S1 action authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: in-band judgetool, repair loop, replanning, task graph/status, score threshold and bounded repair count. - Closure path: execution state -&gt; same operational agent judges/repairs/replans -&gt; same task continues; this closes S1 recovery, not a separate management loop over the current whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven replanning is real discretion, but its organizational function is local task execution/recovery rather than whole-system current control. - Evidence: oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: names such as planner or orchestrating agent are not promoted to S3 without a whole-system current-view/current-control witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party independent complementary-audit channel with distinct access/judgment and corrective return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: FRIDAY evaluates generated code/output and may repair or replan, but this is ordinary in-band operational verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor is evidenced with a separate claim-checking path over operational reality. - Decision owner: none established for S3; judgetool uses the ordinary executor module/model configuration inside the same task loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM judgment prompt, execution result/error/filesystem observations, score threshold and repair/replan path. - Closure path: operation -&gt; same in-band executor/judge evaluates -&gt; same agent repairs/replans -&gt; operation continues; no independent complementary path returns findings to a separate corrective owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic judging exists, but independence/complementary access required for S3 is absent. - Evidence: oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/modules/basemodule.py; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: calling the component a judge or critic does not satisfy S3; no separate reviewer/model/provider/evidence channel comparable to an independent audit path is configured by the standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: generate and implement prospective reusable computer capabilities from external software/package/demo context so later operational tasks can perform work the current repertoire could not. - Disturbance / variety regulated: capability gaps exposed by unfamiliar software/application domains, new packages/APIs and task classes not covered by the existing generated-tool repository. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a prospective course/lesson set for the selected external software/package context and, through lesson execution, generate reusable tools that expand the future action repertoire. - Decision owner: first-party SelfLearner model for course design plus the model-driven FRIDAY lesson execution path that produces qualifying reusable tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: software/package/demo inputs, prior-course history, model-authored course JSON, SelfLearning.learncourse, ordinary FRIDAY task execution, score threshold, persistent generated-tool files/JSON/Chroma vector database and retriever similarity search. - Closure path: operator identifies a capability domain -&gt; learner reads software/package/demo/prior-course context -&gt; model designs prospective lessons -&gt; FRIDAY executes lessons -&gt; successful generated Python capabilities are persisted -&gt; later ordinary task queries the same repository -&gt; newly learned tool descriptions/code are retrieved and influence execution -&gt; previously unsupported task becomes executable. - Boundary reachability: courselearning.py is a shipped first-party entrypoint documented by the self-learning tutorial; no downstream code changes or custom orchestration are required to create and reuse the adapted capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator selects the broad learning target, while the first-party model determines the course/lessons and FRIDAY generates/qualifies the executable tools; deterministic repository code only persists and retrieves those chosen capabilities. - External distinction: software/package context and optional demo-file contents describe capabilities outside FRIDAY&#39;s existing action repertoire; the documented example uses the external openpyxl library and an Excel file. - Future / prospective distinction: the learning mode is explicitly run to acquire tools for later tasks, rather than only repairing the presently failing task; its persisted output survives the learning episode and is queried in subsequent task planning/execution. - Adaptation option generated: a model-authored course of new capability lessons and the reusable generated tools produced by executing those lessons. - Path back into current capability / S3: qualifying tools are written into generatedtools.json, code/description files and the persistent vector index; ordinary FridayRetriever then automatically retrieves them for later S1 task planning/code generation, changing the executable repertoire without repository-source modification. - Evidence: courselearning.py; oscopilot/agents/selflearning.py; oscopilot/modules/learner/selflearner.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/toolrepository/manager/toolmanager.py; oscopilot/modules/retriever/vectorretriever.py; docs/source/tutorials/selflearning.rst. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: self-learning is a separate supported operating mode rather than automatically triggered from every failed task. That affects invocation, not ownership of the adaptation option once the mode is invoked. The broad learning domain/package remains operator-supplied and is not treated as autonomous S5 policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task, model/provider settings, score/repair limits, available tools and self-learning domain inputs constrain behavior, but they are task/configuration/capability controls rather than organizational identity or ultimate-policy decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for defining/reconciling FRIDAY&#39;s identity, ultimate purpose or legitimate highest-level policy under competing operational/adaptation demands. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: environment/configuration, prompts, thresholds, user task/learning inputs and static runtime structure. - Closure path: configuration/task inputs directly constrain S1/S4 behavior; no identity-level issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate authority -&gt; authoritative policy decision -&gt; returned governance loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: FRIDAY exercises operational and capability-adaptation discretion, but not authority to redefine its own organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md; oscopilot/modules/basemodule.py; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; courselearning.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user-supplied objectives/learning targets are parent inputs, but ordinary task/capability target selection is not S5=P without an identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/df12b45707a2cb0c30902dcd8b2df91a67f603c7/assessments/os-copilot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit df12b45707a2cb0c30902dcd8b2df91a67f603c7\">df12b45 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T14:55:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentScope 2.0</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope/tree/5ff52f877de12d66a30d55af279dd4f42b1590f3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5ff52f877de12d66a30d55af279dd4f42b1590f3\">5ff52f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configurable first-party model-directed reasoning/acting tool loop performs user/worker tasks and returns tool/environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown task conditions, tool execution responses/errors, context limits and external model results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose goal-specific tool calls, perform actions, interpret outcomes and continue or finish. - Decision owner: AgentScope-configured agent model in the SDK/Service&#39;s direct agent execution mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model adapters, Toolkit, middleware hooks, workspace/sandbox, permission gates, message/session store. - Closure path: User or leader prompt → agent reasoning → selected tool action → tool/environment result → model continuation and final worker report. - Boundary reachability: The published SDK and example Agent Service initialize real AgentScope Agent instances with toolkit/model and feed messages into replystream (README.md; src/agentscope/agent/agent.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive task acts; host/session middleware only applies technical limits and transport policy. - Evidence: src/agentscope/agent/agent.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py; src/agentscope/tool/toolkit.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate team message routing and session locking, but no proven function-specific first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation with returned feedback. - Distinct S1 units: Separately instantiated model-driven members of a first-party TeamRecord, each with its own session/task and message bus. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Potential conflicting simultaneous operations against shared task resources or overlapping workspace targets; the service&#39;s mere presence of separate sessions does not establish the specific disturbance regulation. - Attenuating coordination relation: The observed session lock serializes calls within one session; TeamSay routes messages and avoids misdelivery, but a function-specific inter-member contention/coordination policy has not been demonstrated. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Team messages and leader reassignments exist; their connection to mitigation of a particular verified inter-worker conflict remains unproven. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: No positive S2 attribution is made from generic team topology, messaging, per-session locks or leader assignment alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate inter-member resource contention, not established as an implemented control target. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved for interference-specific arbitration among independent worker sessions. - Decision owner: Potential leader or host service, not yet reconstructable for a material S2 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MessageBus, TeamSay, sessionrun lock, team membership constraints, toolkit role filtering. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No positive S2 owner is credited; the leader sends messages and the service serializes individual session turns, but neither path is proven to own inter-worker interference-specific decisions. - Closure path: Inter-agent messages are delivered; no defensible concrete collision → arbitration → modified workers path established. - Evidence: src/agentscope/app/tool/teamsay.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/teamcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led team leader makes current team staffing, delegation, repair and follow-up decisions based on worker reports. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pending delegated tasks, changed worker outcomes, failures, information gaps and incomplete task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Create a team, select role/worker, assign task, request clarifications and continue work after returned findings. - Decision owner: First-party hosted model leader using TeamCreate/AgentCreate/TeamSay, not a deterministic background task dispatcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamRecord, role-separated team tools, agent/worker session allocation, message bus, report-enforcing middleware. - Whole-system current view: Leader receives team roster and completion/failure reports of the team it created; no claim of full multi-tenant service-wide control. - Current-control decision scope: Assign current work, change team tasks, re-instruct workers and integrate returned work in the bounded team recursion. - Closure path: Worker performs task and sends mandatory TeamSay report → leader model receives result → chooses follow-up or new task → worker session/action changes. - Boundary reachability: The Agent Service toolkit grants leader-specific team tools in configured leader mode; worker reports are actively enforced by middleware and persisted/routed back to the leader (src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actual choices of child role, scope and continuation are model-generated via shipped team tools; technical message delivery and individual session run locks are merely enforcement. - Evidence: src/agentscope/app/tool/teamcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/teamsay.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: GoalPipeline provides separate executor/verifier agents and revision feedback, but complete independent operational-artifact challenge path is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False executor goal-completion statements, missing required deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Verifier returns pass/fail about executor goal and an explanation, which may prompt another executor iteration. - Decision owner: Separately configured verifier Agent, subject to its actual tools/evidence access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalPipeline executor/verifier alternation, structured execution report, structured verification result. - Claim being audited: Executor&#39;s report that it fulfilled the original goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Executor returns a structured report under ExecutionReport. - Complementary access path: The verifier receives original goal plus executor&#39;s text report; supported independently sampled source/tool artifact inspection is not guaranteed by this pipeline contract. - Independence boundary: Distinct Agent object is possible, but materially independent observation from primary operational artifacts is unresolved. - Who acts on findings: Pipeline sends failure feedback back to executor, conditional on the verifier&#39;s structured judgment. - Closure path: Report → verifier model check → pass/fail → continue or return; missing independent observational basis prevents positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent model call is necessary but not sufficient when its only guaranteed input is the producer&#39;s own claim. - Evidence: src/agentscope/pipeline/goalpipeline.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Persistent memory, scheduling and middleware extension may support reuse across future tasks but do not yet establish an outside-and-then capability adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New task conditions, changing knowledge and external user contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No confirmed owner/loop deciding a prospective adaptation option to future team capability. - Decision owner: Unresolved between user, configured agent, memory backend and developer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Long-term memory middleware, scheduler manager and goal/planning toolkit. - External distinction: External observations may be retained in memory, but this alone is not systemic intelligence. - Future / prospective distinction: Reuse is future-facing in time but not necessarily prospective organizational adaptation. - Adaptation option generated: Not established from the read-only source evidence. - Path back into current capability / S3: Memory/context or schedule modifications can affect future runs; full future intelligence → change is unproven. - Closure path: Captured memory → later prompt is technically supported, but no established S4 choice/feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory retention and schedules are mechanisms, not proof of autonomous or parent S4. - Evidence: src/agentscope/middleware/longtermmemory; src/agentscope/app/manager/scheduler/schedulermanager.py; src/agentscope/tool/task/updatetask.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Team identities, access/permission rules and human confirmations exist without established ultimate-policy identity decision/return loop at the bounded team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permissions over tools, resource access, multi-tenant rights and delegation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The human/application owner sets permission rules and may approve actions, but ultimate organizational identity/policy closure is unresolved. - Decision owner: User/app role policies; not automatically a first-party agent policy controller or a qualified parent S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionEngine, TeamRecord roles, workspace permissions and HITL middleware. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Allowed identity, roles and acceptable operating permissions of configured team members. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No S5-specific autonomous/parent owner established; execution-level rules are narrower than ultimate policy. - Return-to-operation path: Permission decisions guard specific tool calls and inheritance to child agents, not a proven changing-policy feedback loop. - Closure path: Permission request/policy → allow/deny action, lacking ultimate identity-level decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A policy engine enforces rules but does not decide the system&#39;s ultimate purpose or identity. - Evidence: src/agentscope/permission/engine.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/44d6c86f16e424c65ea333f05cf07553822f7db5/assessments/agentscope-2-0.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 44d6c86f16e424c65ea333f05cf07553822f7db5\">44d6c86 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T22:21:05Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/letta-ai/letta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Letta</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/tree/5bcdd177d70fa2b31a754cfcd801e77b2e1ab16a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5bcdd177d70fa2b31a754cfcd801e77b2e1ab16a\">5bcdd17 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary agent owns model/tool action decisions and can delegate bounded work to first-party subagents.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: subagent launch, parallel execution and lifecycle tracking do not establish an autonomous anti-oscillation/shared-resource coordination decision right among peer S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the subagent manager schedules/delegates tasks but does not own whole-system current priorities, resources and commitments as a superior regulator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the default compaction-triggered reflection path launches a distinct autonomous background reviewer over already-completed primary-agent conversations. It searches for mistakes, contradictions and durable corrections and has an automatic integration path into parent memory/skills. This is a separate complementary audit/correction loop rather than ordinary self-reflection inside the S1 turn.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: reflection is retrospective internal learning from the agent&#39;s own conversations. It does not establish a distinct outside-and-then environmental intelligence function coupled to strategic adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: reflection may maintain memory/persona/skills, but ultimate identity, policy and authority remain constrained by parent/runtime configuration; editable memory is not legitimate S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0/assessments/letta.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0\">ae8749a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MetaGPT</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/tree/11cdf466d042aece04fc6cfd13b28e1a70341b1f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 11cdf466d042aece04fc6cfd13b28e1a70341b1f\">11cdf46 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: specialized role agents autonomously produce requirements, designs, code, analyses and other operational artifacts.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the reviewed leader/SOP path primarily assigns and sequences work. No separate evidence shows a function that dampens destructive interference or oscillation among autonomous S1 units without centralizing their decisions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the default TeamLeader has a whole-team current view through team information, routed feedback and a structured plan; it can regulate current commitments by assigning specialists, closing tasks, changing/resetting the plan and gating downstream work on prerequisite results. This goes beyond worker selection alone and is exercised by an autonomous shipped role. Parent-authored roles, SOPs, prompts, budget and team composition constrain this loop but do not provide a separate first-party human/operator decision path over the same current whole-team commitments, so no (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: review/testing inside the normal software-production path does not provide a materially independent alternative access channel to operational reality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: planning, product/competitive artifacts and replanning are project work; the reviewed runtime does not establish a distinct external-and-prospective adaptation loop in two-way conversation with S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: role definitions, SOP, prompts, budget and team composition are parent/developer authored. No runtime agent holds legitimate ultimate identity/policy closure for the system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/metagpt.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T23:12:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LobeHub</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub/tree/3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\">3af256c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialized agents autonomously perform assigned conversational, research, creation or tool-using work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user tasks, domain-specific information, tool/model observations and task-specific uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive actions and output needed for the agent&#39;s assigned task or conversational role. - Decision owner: the invoked model-driven agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent configuration, system role, model/provider selection, plugins, isolated async task context and session state. - Closure path: agent receives an instruction, performs work, returns a result to the group/user, and that result becomes input to subsequent orchestration or conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-driven agent owns substantive task execution while LobeHub transports context/tools/results. - Evidence: README.md, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: coordinate multiple specialized S1 agents by choosing dependent versus parallel work, agent selection, handoff order and group interaction mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-sensitive work that would be wrong in parallel, redundant/overlapping contributions, poor handoffs and unstructured multi-agent interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to use one agent, broadcast, sequential speaking, one async task or multiple parallel tasks, and choose the participating agents/instructions. - Decision owner: the model-driven Group Supervisor / Orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: speak, broadcast, executeAgentTask, executeAgentTasks, group orchestration runtime, shared group context and supervisor resume after member completion. - Closure path: supervisor analyzes the request, selects a coordination pattern and dispatches agents; member results return to the supervisor, which synthesizes, follows up or changes the next interaction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime executes the selected pattern, but the supervisor model owns the coordination discretion over mode, agent selection and follow-up. - Evidence: packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, group orchestration runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic callAgent alone would not establish S2; the positive mapping depends on the explicit dependency/parallelism decision framework and returned supervisor loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialized agents in an Agent Group, each capable of separate operational work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: dependent subtasks can be run in the wrong order, overlapping agents can add redundant work, and independent subtasks can be unnecessarily serialized; first-party guidance also calls out weak handoffs and overlapping roles as collaboration failures. - Attenuating coordination relation: the Group Supervisor chooses sequential, parallel, broadcast or focused modes and assigns work to agents according to dependency and expertise. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: member outputs resume the supervisor; it can ask follow-ups, synthesize, invoke another agent or terminate the group turn, thereby changing later contributions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party supervisor policy explicitly diagnoses dependence/parallelizability and selects an attenuation pattern for interactions among distinct operational agents, with returned member results driving further coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-group current-control function over resources, commitments, priorities or constraints is established beyond the S2 orchestration itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group task routing, model choice and current conversation state were inspected, but no distinct S3 current-control loop is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found with a whole-system current view and separate authority over group-wide commitments/resources/accountability. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent management, group profile editing, async task execution, scheduling and supervisor orchestration. - Closure path: no qualifying distinct S3 loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator&#39;s evidenced rights are inter-agent task/mode coordination for the current group operation; naming it supervisor/operator is not enough to promote that same function to S3. - Evidence: group-management system role, Agent Group docs, agent-management tools. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: review another agent&#39;s produced work through a distinct reviewer/editor role and return critique into revision/re-review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete, unclear, inconsistent or low-quality outputs that require a second perspective before acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge produced work, issue feedback and require/refine another contribution. - Decision owner: constructor path; LobeHub supplies explicit iterative/reviewer group patterns, but the developer/user must compose the reviewer role, independence and authority in the concrete group. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iterative Agent Group mode, distinct agent identities, sequential handoffs, shared context and supervisor-mediated repeated turns. - Closure path: producer emits work; reviewer/editor returns critique; producer revises; reviewer can perform final review in the documented iterative pattern. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate model-driven reviewer can own the judgment once composed, but the standard distribution does not assign an invariant independent reviewer or audit authority automatically. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: ordinary debate or synthesis is not counted as S3; the positive constructor claim is limited to the explicitly documented review/refine pattern with distinct roles. - Claim being audited: that another agent&#39;s draft/output is sufficiently correct, clear or complete for the group&#39;s purpose. - Ordinary reporting path: producer agent returns its draft/result through the normal group turn. - Complementary access path: a separately configured Editor/reviewer/QA agent receives the produced work and can critique it in an iterative group workflow. - Independence boundary: first-party group mechanics preserve distinct agent identities, but organizational independence/authority of the reviewer is supplied by the group composition rather than guaranteed by runtime policy. - Who acts on findings: the producing agent receives reviewer feedback and revises; the group/supervisor can route the revised result back for final review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/prospective organizational intelligence loop is established at the Agent Group boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preference/history changes are learned, but no distinct future/environment adaptation function is closed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found that senses external/future distinctions, generates adaptation options and returns a selected change into group capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Personal Memory, editable structured memory, continual learning and tool/search capability. - Closure path: memory changes later agent context directly; no separate outside-and-then option-development loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: personalization/learning and external tools do not automatically establish S4. - Evidence: README.md, Agent Group/runtime docs. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy governance function is established for the Agent Group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group prompt, member roster and agent configuration can change, but no ultimate identity dispute/authority loop is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity-level authoritative decision with a return-to-operation closure was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: group profile, Agent Builder, agent CRUD, prompts, skills and user configuration. - Closure path: configuration edits alter operation, but they are not an evidenced S5 identity/ultimate-policy process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and editable configuration are implementation/control artifacts, not proof of VSM S5. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0/assessments/lobehub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0\">9f83e41 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:19:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Swarms</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms/tree/3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\">3009364 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed individual first-party Agent solves a task via model/tool choice, observation and iterative continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dynamic goals, changing tool outcomes, external model output, context and task errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose steps, call tools/subagents and decide when to continue or finish an operating task. - Decision owner: Per-agent hosted LLM/Agent runtime, not merely an agent-shaped tuple invoked by a deterministic sequencer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent run, runautonomousloop, tool executor, model wrapper and workspace. - Closure path: Task → model-generated action/tool use → environment result → next model reasoning and eventual completion. - Boundary reachability: Python Agent.run and optional autonomous-loop configuration are used from the published distribution (README.md; swarms/structs/agent.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context-sensitive model actions select operating acts; loop counters and provider wrappers do not own that choice. - Evidence: swarms/structs/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: In bounded GroupChat mode, mediate multiple model-agent bids competing for a single shared conversational speaking slot, preventing speaker domination while retaining useful contributions. - Distinct S1 units: A configured GroupChat.agents set of independently invoked Agent objects, each producing a context-contingent (score, reply) via model-backed respond tool. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Several operating agents seek the same shared conversation floor simultaneously; unchecked publication produces competing turns, and repeated high-frequency speakers dominate the shared information channel. - Attenuating coordination relation: collectbids calls each agent, selectspeaker applies a shared threshold and recency penalty, chooses exactly one speaker, and post updates common conversation history seen by all peers on the next turn. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The selected speaker&#39;s contribution, not all conflicting bids, enters shared history for every agent&#39;s following model turn; recent speakers are penalized in future selections. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit gate is driven by simultaneous claims to a scarce common conversational medium and recurring dominance, with a fairness attenuation policy and returned altered information environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting simultaneous bids, message crowding and disproportionate conversational control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select exactly one admissible turn among competing agent contributions. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic GroupChat floor arbiter, therefore C, not a separate agentic S2 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: respond(score,message) tool, threshold, recency window/penalty, chosen-speaker posting and shared Conversation. - Closure path: Independently formed bids → contention/fairness gate → one allowed published contribution → next turn&#39;s changed conversation and adjusted future bids. - Boundary reachability: GroupChat.run uses the packaged bidding, selecting and posting operations; this is an opt-in published architecture, not an inactive helper (swarms/structs/groupchat.py; README.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents determine proposed content and scores; the decisive inter-agent floor grant is deterministic host policy (C). - Evidence: swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/conversation.py; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Applies to GroupChat&#39;s shared conversation medium, not arbitrary shared file changes or all Swarms topologies. Does not claim broad resource conflict arbitration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led HierarchicalSwarm director assigns present worker orders and adjusts ongoing team work from collected subordinate results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Divergent worker progress/results, uncertain task division, errors and changing completion quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Produce a model-derived order batch, choose which specialist performs what, inspect returned outputs and issue fresh orders in further loops. - Decision owner: First-party configured model director; deterministic parseorders and executeorders relay its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HierarchicalSwarm director, OrderBatch parser, worker dispatch and optional feedback director, conversation storage. - Whole-system current view: Director sees the bounded swarm conversation/worker results and prior execution history, not every organization using Swarms. - Current-control decision scope: Current worker allocation, ordering, revisions and reattempts for the HierarchicalSwarm instance. - Closure path: Director plans and orders → specialized workers run → results return and enter conversation → director next loop reassigns/replans. - Boundary reachability: HierarchicalSwarm.run calls step, rundirector, parseorders, executeorders, and loops over result feedback when maxloops allows (swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual work orders originate in a model-led director within the shipped topology; loop counters and dispatcher enforce, not replace, task choices. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Some prebuilt modes include judge/verification or consensus phases; materially independent operating evidence inspection and correction path remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Invalid subordinate claims, low-quality task completion or conflicting agent assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge can evaluate outputs; whether it has independent primary-source evidence and authority over correction is not guaranteed. - Decision owner: Configured judge/consensus agent, not established as a truly separately observing auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hierarchical judge toggle, MajorityVoting consensus and HeavySwarm verification-oriented roles. - Claim being audited: Specialist worker answer/output claims. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker results added to director conversation, majority-voting conversation or final report. - Complementary access path: Shipped judging paths chiefly pass worker outputs/derived conversation text; a separate direct observation of work artifacts has not been established at the first-party boundary. - Independence boundary: A different model/persona is not a guaranteed independent observations channel. - Who acts on findings: Directors or consensus can summarize and reconsider, but an independent audit finding → corrective operational reroute is unproven. - Closure path: Output → judge/consensus verdict → report or next loop, insufficient to classify a distinct S3 audit under the governing method. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Another LLM rating peer output is not equivalent to a complementary function sampling reality independently. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/majorityvoting.py; swarms/structs/heavyswarm.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Planning prompts, persistent workspace and memory may help later tasks but a decision owner adapting future systemic capability from external prospective observations has not been established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future requirements, market or environment shifts and changing task complexity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved among user-supplied swarm config, model director and persisted task memory. - Decision owner: No uniquely identified first-party outside-and-then intelligence controller at the bounded swarm recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Conversation/workspace storage, planning role prompts, dynamic agent layouts. - External distinction: Outside facts can enter model context, but information access alone is not S4. - Future / prospective distinction: Multi-loop plans address current task commitments, not shown to adapt future organization capability. - Adaptation option generated: No verified capability-evolution choice produced by a distinct owner. - Path back into current capability / S3: Persistence and plan prompts can affect later runs, but external foresight → applied organizational redesign remains unresolved. - Closure path: Retained context → later work, incomplete future-intelligence and capability-change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompted planning and memory are not enough for S4. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/utils/workspacemanager.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Configured swarm identity, roles and execution limits exist but an ultimate organizational purpose/policy closure is not traced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User boundaries, acceptable agent/tool behavior and system-level authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer/user chooses prompts, members and parameters; no proven ultimate-policy choice by a qualified first-party actor. - Decision owner: User-supplied configuration for role identity and deterministic safety checks; a complete parent policy mode remains unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, task role configuration and tool/platform restrictions. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Who chooses and revises the organization&#39;s overarching identity, purpose and final authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Not established beyond out-of-band user/developer configuration. - Return-to-operation path: Prompt and parameter selection guides run behavior, but no complete governing policy feedback is proven. - Closure path: Configured goals/roles → worker execution; no verified policy challenge/ultimate revision returned to operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A system prompt or fixed role roster is not itself an ultimate decision loop. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800/assessments/swarms.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800\">5b1d5a9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:31:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CAMEL Workforce</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/tree/8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\">8c791b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Worker agents autonomously execute assigned task work through model/tool loops.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: C. Workforce maintains explicit cross-task dependency state and gates release/order of work across multiple workers. This prevents dependent S1 work from proceeding against unsatisfied predecessor commitments, establishing a concrete coordination path beyond routing. The mechanism is supplied deterministically by Workforce while the application supplies the task graph, so ownership is constructor-side rather than agent-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The Coordinator operates over shared workforce state, assigns work based on worker capability, tracks current commitments, handles failures, and can create new workers or redirect/replan work. These are whole-workforce current-control rights over assignments and available operational capacity. The model-driven coordinator owns the runtime choice within configured bounds.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Quality/failure analysis evaluates returned task results in the ordinary Workforce control/reporting path. No materially complementary evidence-access channel or protected independent audit boundary was established. The historical C therefore does not survive current S3 criteria.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Replanning and dynamic worker creation respond to current-task failure; they do not establish an external/future environmental intelligence loop that changes organizational capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human pause/stop/skip controls and the externally supplied objective are parent/operator controls over ordinary execution, not a demonstrated ultimate-policy/identity closure path. The historical P is therefore removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4/assessments/camel-workforce.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4\">cfe2730 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T19:14:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go Micro</a><small>2015</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro/tree/792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\">792b18c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/task state through a model → tool/service/delegation → observation loop until an operational result is produced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, changing service/tool availability, tool/RPC/A2A results, persistent conversation state, provider responses, tool failures and intermediate observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next service/custom/built-in tool, decide whether to plan or delegate, consume returned observations, continue the loop or settle the answer. - Decision owner: the active Go Micro LLM agent in NewAgent or micro chat direct-service mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model adapters, service registry/tool discovery, built-in plan/delegate/request-input tools, memory/store, tool wrappers, step/loop/spend limits, approvals, checkpointing, retry/timeout logic, RPC/A2A/MCP integration. - Closure path: user/assigned task + current context → agent selects action/tool/delegation → Go Micro executes/gates it → tool/service/sub-agent result returns into model context → agent selects another action or final result. - Boundary reachability: NewAgent and micro chat are shipped, documented first-party runtime modes; the positive S1 loop is implemented in agent/ and CLI runtime code rather than inferred from examples or repository dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven agent while retaining registry, memory, tool execution and guardrails leaves no contextual actor choosing the next operational action. Deterministic wrappers constrain and transport the model&#39;s decisions rather than replacing them. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, cmd/micro/chat/chat.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: external model inference and remote A2A peers are substrate/environment. The positive state is established independently by the first-party agent loop around those dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific loop was established that regulates a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation among distinct Go Micro S1 agent units at the declared product-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included multiple registered agents, RPC/A2A handoffs, service discovery/load balancing, durable flows, duplicate delegate/retry protection and shared registry/store infrastructure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S2-specific decision right is supplied. Delegation selects another actor for a subtask; service discovery/load balancing routes calls; idempotency/retry controls duplicate side effects; flow edges order work. - Decision owner: none established for S2 in the reviewed standard product-runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry, client load balancing, broker/store, plan/delegate tools, A2A/MCP transport, flow sequencing, retry/idempotency guards and isolated ephemeral sub-agent context. - Closure path: no first-party path was found that detects a specific interaction disturbance among two distinct S1 agents, selects an attenuation response for that disturbance, and feeds the coordination result back into subsequent local S1 behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multi-agent reachability, routing and delegation are present, but the Methodology explicitly requires more than communication/handoff. The reviewed product runtime does not supply an S2-specific autonomous or constructor path tied to a concrete inter-S1 oscillation/conflict witness. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, gateway/a2a/a2a.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function was established beyond local agent planning/delegation and deterministic runtime enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate current-control paths inspected included store-backed plans, delegation, flow execution state, registry discovery, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits and runtime stop/resume mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the ordinary agent may revise its own task plan and delegate subtasks, but no separate product-runtime path was found with a whole-system current view plus authority to bargain/regulate shared resources, commitments or priorities on behalf of multiple operational units. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan memory, delegate routing, flow state/checkpoints, registry/client machinery, tool/step/loop/spend limits, approvals and cancellation/timeouts. - Closure path: no S3-specific whole-system feedback/intervention loop was established. Local plan updates return to the same agent&#39;s task execution; deterministic limits enforce preselected constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a parent agent that decomposes one user request and delegates one subtask is not automatically S3. The reviewed evidence does not show a standard mode where an agent receives a whole-system operational view and owns ongoing resource/commitment intervention across independent S1s. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, README.md, flow/. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: .github/loop has project-level planning/current-work behavior, but it belongs to the excluded repository-development organization and is not borrowed into the product-runtime S3 mapping.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a flow-step result through a distinct verification judgment and return rejection feedback into another operational attempt before the flow proceeds with the bounded result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but deficient step outputs that ordinary body execution would otherwise return without a separate grade-and-correction pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Grader decides pass/fail and supplies corrective feedback; Verify threads rejection feedback into the next body attempt and records the final verification outcome. - Decision owner: Go Micro does not ship a sufficiently independent default autonomous auditor for the general case. LLMGrader reuses the flow model and only sees the latest result. The application must compose a grader with independence appropriate to the audited claim; Go Micro supplies the S3-specific verification/feedback closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded Verify attempt loop, feedback-field injection, optional backoff, verification metadata, flow checkpoint/result state and optional LLMGrader helper. - Closure path: body produces candidate result → composed grader audits it → rejection/feedback enters the next body input → body retries → accepted or bounded-failure result becomes the flow state. - Claim being audited: whether the current flow-step output satisfies the deployment-defined rubric/ground truth before it is accepted as the step&#39;s result. - Ordinary reporting path: the body step returns its State as normal flow output. - Complementary access path: the first-party Grader seam invokes a separately composed grading actor after body completion; the deployment can give that actor external ground truth, raw artifacts or a distinct model/service, and Go Micro closes its verdict back into retry. - Independence boundary: independence is not guaranteed by the built-in LLMGrader; it is intentionally left to the developer-supplied Grader. This is why the state is C, not A. - Who acts on findings: flow.Verify deterministically reinjects grader feedback into the next body attempt; the operational body/agent then produces the corrected candidate. - Boundary reachability: flow.Verify, the Grader contract and feedback/retry closure are shipped public first-party flow primitives at the reviewed revision, not CI-only tests. A deployment only needs to supply the independent grading actor/ground-truth access. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific audit path and return loop are first-party, but the decisive sufficiently independent audit actor is not supplied in a standard autonomous mode. General custom-code extensibility is not the basis; the credit is for the dedicated Verify/Grader primitive whose semantics are verification judgment plus corrective return. - Evidence: flow/verify.go, flow/verifytest.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: same-model LLMGrader alone is not treated as sufficient complementary independence. A deployment that wires only that helper may not operationally realize S3 even though the first-party constructor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: detect that current operational capability cannot satisfy an external requirement, develop a service-level adaptation, materialize it, and return the new capability into subsequent agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user needs not covered by existing registered services/tools; requests to extend or modify an existing system; design/code/compile failures encountered while materializing the capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base mode — the micro chat agent decides that no existing service can handle the request and chooses microgenerateservice; the generation models choose the service architecture/handler changes. Parent mode — the user reviews the LLM-designed system proposal and decides at Generate? [Y/n] whether the adaptation should be materialized. - Decision owner: Base mode — the autonomous chat/generation model path. Parent mode — the human operator owns the decisive accept/reject adaptation judgment, while the generator autonomously develops and implements the approved option. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry/tool discovery, generate.Design, existing-service scan, deterministic service-structure generation, LLM handler generation, bounded compile-fix loop, process startup, registry wait and tool refresh. - Closure path: external capability request + current service inventory → missing-capability distinction → model designs service adaptation → autonomous choice or parent approval triggers generation → code is written/compile-fixed/built/started → registry/tool set refreshes → subsequent agent turns can use the new service. - External distinction: the user asks for a capability absent from the current registered service/tool environment, or asks to extend/modify the existing system. - Future / prospective distinction: Go Micro models a desired future system/service capability relative to the current inventory rather than only selecting among existing tools; Design explicitly receives existing services and constructs the complete extended target design. - Adaptation option generated: one or more new/modified Go Micro services with schema/endpoints, generated handler/business logic and an updated managing agent/tool surface. - Path back into current capability / S3: generated services are compiled and started, allowed to register, micro chat refreshes discovered tools, and the new endpoints become immediately usable by the same operational agent; in initial generation mode the approved system becomes the subsequent running service/agent environment. - Boundary reachability: both micro chat autonomous missing-capability generation and micro run --prompt / CLI user-approved generation are shipped documented CLI modes. The positive mapping does not depend on repository CI or .github/loop dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode removing the model&#39;s missing-capability judgment/service-design choices leaves only a generator with no contextual adaptation decision. In parent mode the human decides whether the proposed adaptation is admitted, and that decision is returned through the same generator into executable capability. - Evidence: cmd/micro/chat/chat.go, cmd/micro/cli/generate/generate.go, cmd/micro/run/run.go, README.md, flow/analyze.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: the adaptation path is developer/runtime oriented rather than production hot-patching of an arbitrary deployed service; the credited mode is nevertheless first-party and explicitly returns generated service capability into the supported running micro chat environment. flow.Analyze/LLMOptimizer alone would be only a partial adaptation constructor because applying its prompt proposal is caller-owned.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no product-runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included agent system prompts, auth/rules, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits, requestinput, generated-service confirmation and repository North Star/maintainer restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none of the product-runtime mechanisms escalates a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that decision to govern subsequent operation at this recursion. - Decision owner: none established for S5 in the assessed product runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auth rules/JWT wrappers, guardrails, tool approvals, static prompts/configuration, human input pauses, spend budgets and generation confirmation. - Closure path: ordinary task/action approvals and configuration changes can alter execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy text, permission checks and human approvals bound ordinary operations; they do not by themselves own system identity or ultimate policy. Repository NORTHSTAR and off-limits maintainer decisions belong to the excluded repository-development organization. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, auth/, internal/docs/CONTINUOUSIMPROVEMENT.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a/assessments/go-micro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a\">9a9faa2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T18:36:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Balanov/uncut\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNCUT</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Balanov/uncut/tree/e1512a702de846eb701e0383d9ae031bad528c0f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e1512a702de846eb701e0383d9ae031bad528c0f\">e1512a7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete a user-requested coding/investigation outcome through a model-chosen sequence of workspace actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source organization, edit/test outcomes, command failures and incomplete information discovered while implementing the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and revise file/shell operations from returned evidence, decide whether more work is needed, and issue the final result. - Decision owner: the configured model-backed coding agent in Runner.Run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go call loop, four tool implementations, workspace path guard, provider adapter, session log, step budget, timeout and output-error handling. - Closure path: task prompt → model tool call → Executor.Execute → tool result appended to message history → next model judgment/action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: the standard uncut CLI constructs Runner with provider and executor and invokes Runner.Run; no dogfood-only or sample integration is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: with the model removed the runtime can expose tools and enforce limits but cannot select substantive edits or interpret feedback into task completion. - Evidence: main.go, run.go, tools.go, runtest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the wired model/tool feedback path; live provider task success is not claimed. - Caveats: model quality and credentials are external; the shell can act broadly within OS permissions despite the file-tool workspace check.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination among distinct operating S1 units is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no shipped peer-agent interference or oscillation is identified at this single-agent boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 adjustment. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential tool-call execution and one shared conversation/session. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing multiple tool calls from one model is not the coordination of distinct autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: run.go, types.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external shell commands could launch independent agents, but UNCUT does not ship and coordinate such a peer organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system supervisory current-control loop exists above the one S1 agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-run step/time limits and tool errors are bounded locally, without whole-system resource/commitment management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for substantive cross-unit current control. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: --max-steps, HTTP timeout, context cancellation and error exits. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator preselects limits and the runtime enforces them; the coding agent does not gain a whole-system live view or authority to reallocate independent units&#39; commitments. - Evidence: main.go, run.go. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a single S1 agent can choose its next local action; that is not by itself a higher-recursion S3 decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary audit channel is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: file/test evidence may be inspected by the ordinary coding agent, but no separate complementary audit challenges its reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an independent audit judgment. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: readfile, runcommand, full tool-result logging and optional NDJSON observation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same S1 model may run tests or read diffs; these ordinary feedback operations and emitted logs do not supply an independent auditor and corrective-return decision. - Evidence: tools.go, run.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an external operator can audit emitted events or instruct a separate reviewer, but such an auditor is not a shipped first-party organizational path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes in future tools/models/environment are left to external operator choices, rather than modeled by a first-party capability-adaptation process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for future-oriented adaptation options. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reusable session messages, configurable provider URL/model, system instructions and AGENTS.md loading. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conversation continuation, current-task replanning and manual provider selection do not develop externally grounded future options and return them into durable present capability. - Evidence: main.go, session.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user could ask for strategic research as an ordinary task, but the standard harness lacks a continuing S4 feedback mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is closed at the running harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operating instructions and tool scope constrain the coding task, but identity-level conflict is not routed to authoritative governance and returned. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established at ultimate-policy level. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static default system prompt, optional workspace AGENTS.md, CLI choices, file path validation and hard limits. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator sets instructions and limits, the Go runtime enforces tool semantics, and no shipped identity-policy proposal/decision/return route exists; a generic human task instruction is not S5 parent closure. - Evidence: main.go, tools.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository-maintainer policy/release decisions belong to an adjacent OSS development organization rather than this CLI session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e97a86e2c5b160b500b66d88d5fc92f524433c62/assessments/un/uncut.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e97a86e2c5b160b500b66d88d5fc92f524433c62\">e97a86e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:25:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peristyle-ai/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peristyle Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peristyle-ai/harness/tree/cfa54f2c808058d2372521c53019f6f3e4b5a52c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cfa54f2c808058d2372521c53019f6f3e4b5a52c\">cfa54f2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform requested chat, research, memory and machine tasks through an adaptive sequence of model-chosen tool calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable user requests and unexpected shell, SQL, memory and API results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each named LLM agent chooses its next tool, arguments, continuation or answer using prior observations. - Decision owner: the instantiated model-driven agent in AgentLoop; not the Flask router, deterministic safety classifier or model provider as an organizational unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent bootstrap/DB isolation, tool schemas, context window, iteration/wall-time limits, semaphore, persisted trajectory and safety confirmations. - Closure path: tool output is returned into the model&#39;s subsequent context; the next action or final text is persisted and delivered to the caller. - Boundary reachability: the standard Flask chat endpoint and group dispatcher call the shipping AgentLoop directly; this does not depend on development CI or an external agent framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous agent has substantive discretion within the user task; deterministic guards and operator confirmation constrain rather than choose those operational actions. - Evidence: app/agent/loop.py#L667-L755; app/agent/loop.py#L758-L829; app/agent/loop.py#L910-L1020; app/agent/tools/registry.py#L1-L120. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: high for source-reachable execution; no provider-backed E2E. - Caveats: effectful shell commands can require user confirmation and model availability; those do not imply general agent sovereignty.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no established S2-specific regulatory loop for interference/oscillation among independently acting named S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially inconsistent multi-agent replies or shared task interference; the repo documents transcript order, not an actual systemic conflict and its corrective regulation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none shown for regulating that inter-S1 disturbance; @mention order selects who speaks and whose reply enters later context. - Decision owner: deterministic group dispatcher, not an autonomous coordination agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: roster filter, sequential dispatch, shared transcript, per-agent schemas and history overrides. - Closure path: previous replies appear in later prompts, but no first-party conflict detection, damping decision, revised joint commitments or stabilization feedback is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: communication and serialization are not S2 solely because distinct agents participate. - Evidence: app/agent/groupchat.py#L1-L29; app/agent/groupchat.py#L46-L76; app/agent/groupchat.py#L141-L273; app/agent/loop.py#L713-L755. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: independently configured agents may cooperate via text, but the standard path lacks a function-specific inter-S1 disturbance witness. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no discretionary whole-harness current-control loop over its multiple named agents established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local model latency, turn duration, memory budget and concurrent LLM demand; regulated by preconfigured enforcement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent- or parent-owned whole-system tradeoff among running units, priorities, shared budgets and commitments. - Decision owner: operator preselects config; counters/semaphore, per-turn protection, and guards enforce it without ongoing S3 judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM semaphore, per-turn rate/iteration caps, context trimming, group serial dispatcher, UI agent picker, config per-agent model selection. - Closure path: a turn is delayed/stopped/trimmed; no observed aggregate present-state feedback reassigns resources or priorities across the agent collective. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operator configuration and deterministic scheduling alone do not constitute whole-system S3 ownership. - Evidence: app/agent/loop.py#L803-L835; app/agent/loop.py#L910-L940; app/agent/groupchat.py#L208-L240; app/config.py#L111-L161. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an administrator can intervene manually but no first-party ongoing parent-controlled S3 closure was reconstructed. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no operationally separate, sufficiently independent audit judgment that feeds corrective control to the operational collective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: hallucinated tool claims, command safety, faulty turns and other local execution errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent audit channel with own access and judgment; most guards inspect the same generated text/tool inputs. - Decision owner: deterministic runtime rules and human-facing diagnostics, rather than a distinct audit actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: intent-without-execution nudges, protected path detection, shell audit log, last-prompt/last-emission admin routes and interruption recording. - Closure path: error nudges can return to the same operational agent; diagnostics can inform an operator, but neither is independent operational-reality audit/feedback into parent control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: observing or blocking an agent&#39;s own proposed command is not complementary independent audit. - Evidence: app/agent/loop.py#L49-L164; app/agent/tools/execbash.py#L1-L33; app/routes/api.py#L945-L1006; app/agent/turnguard.py#L159-L282. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: operator log review is possible outside the automatic harness path. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no established externally sensing, future-oriented adaptation loop changing the harness&#39;s capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring memory pressure and recent-turn friction, which are internal context-maintenance phenomena. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reverie makes local memory cleanup choices and suggests future proposals; no first-party external opportunity/threat judgment is adopted into capability strategy. - Decision owner: agent controls cleanup; any wider changes remain future user requests and manual work outside a demonstrated S4 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: nightly timer, activity gate, last-50-turn reflection, file/memory targets, report to user. - Closure path: local context is tidied and next-turn bootstrap refreshed; this improves operational continuity, not outside-and-then adaptation of the system&#39;s repertoire. Proposed future work has no provided adoption/implementation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reflecting, optimizing internal memory and generating proposals does not alone establish S4. - Evidence: app/agent/reverie.py#L1-L45; app/agent/reverie.py#L135-L305; app/agent/reverie.py#L396-L428; app/routes/api.py#L376-L539. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: operator-directed future upgrades might create an S4 organization, but are not evidenced as standard-distribution closure. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether the installed agent&#39;s core SOUL.md behavioral constitution may be changed during unattended maintenance. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the legitimate human operator alone in the configured reverie parent-governed mode; no autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: operator-approved protected identity edit is reread by BootstrapRegistry before the next agent turn and injected into the model&#39;s bootstrap policy. - Function: reserve ultimate agent identity and protected policy changes to the human operator while allowing the running agent to surface a proposal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: pressure to let a self-maintaining agent rewrite its own core behavioral boundaries during an unattended nightly turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve/reject or make the authoritative change to the protected SOUL.md core rules; the agent must propose rather than execute that change in reverie. - Decision owner: human user/operator as legitimate parent; the agent can identify and report a proposed change but does not own that ultimate right in this mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: protectedfiles config, reverie brief/system-scope, execbash write block, persisted chat report and BootstrapRegistry refresh. - Closure path: reverie raises identity proposals in the operator&#39;s main conversation; operator may grant permission and edit the protected file; the standard bootstrap loader rereads it at the next turn and injects the changed rules into subsequent agent decisions. A rejected/no-change proposal leaves existing policy in force. - Boundary reachability: this parent-governed mode is shipped in the normal optional reverie route, standard shell tool and next-turn bootstrap reload; the core SOUL.md is an installed identity file. No external plugin, contributor workflow or imagined parent process is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the protected path explicitly removes unilateral agent authority on identity, while the operator controls whether policy changes and the live runtime consumes the resulting identity. - Evidence: agenttemplate/SOUL.md.template#L1-L51; app/agent/reverie.py#L279-L295; app/routes/api.py#L491-L518; app/agent/tools/execbash.py#L229-L256; app/agent/bootstrap.py#L101-L155; app/agent/bootstrap.py#L275-L301. - Basis: explicit / structural parent-governed closure. - Confidence: medium for the supported path; no observed real operator acceptance or production E2E claimed. - Caveats: this P claim is restricted to the protected identity-policy path in configured reverie mode, not all arbitrary user prompts, ordinary confirmation requests, static safety rules or OSS governance. The manual operator approval/edit step is the parent decision; mere editing ability without the shipped escalation/return chain would not suffice.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6855685af60e624ee1ce41d664ef6c8f9fecafaa/assessments/pe/peristyle-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6855685af60e624ee1ce41d664ef6c8f9fecafaa\">6855685 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
